Clouds the color of wet slate hung heavy over Helvanthor, swallowing the spires of the upper city and leaving the lower market in a dull, sunless gloom. Canvas awnings sagged between crooked poles, streaked brown and green by old rain, shading stalls of bruised turnips, fish gone soft at the gills, and secondhand boots worn through at the soles. Runoff from a hundred gutters slicked the cobbles, and its sour stink rose into the smoke of the cookfires.

Dusty leaned against the rough-hewn post of the bread stall, his hood pulled low, his cloak hanging stiff with grime. Rags layered beneath it, one over another, frayed ends of gray and brown poking out at his wrists and collar. He stood a head above most of the market, so he bent, his back rounded, folding his long frame down into the awning's shadow.

The smell of him still turned heads.

"Gods, boy." Old Tobin fanned a floury hand in front of his face, his nose wrinkling above a beard gone the gray of ash. "You'll turn my dough before it rises."

"I'm sorry, sir." Dusty kept his black eyes on the crowd. "I can stand farther off, if you'd like. I won't be able to see the corner by the cooper's from there."

Tobin grumbled into his beard and turned his loaves so their browned crusts faced the street. Small, dense loaves, dark with rye and flecked with chaff.

At the far end of the square two city watchmen slouched beneath dented half-helms, their red tabards faded to the pink of old meat, halberds propped against the well house. One chewed an apple he had not paid for. The other watched the woman bent over the washing trough.

Dusty saw the elf first.

She came in from the northern lane, and the chatter at each stall died as she passed, picking up again low and hushed behind her. Her pale pink hair was woven into a thick braid that swung past her waist, loose wisps curling at her temples in the damp air. A green cloak hung from her shoulders, fine wool thinned at the elbows, its hem dark with mud. Beneath it a thick woolen blouse climbed to her throat and hugged every curve of her body, and baggy traveling pants were tucked into heeled brown boots that clicked against the stones.

Two men followed her, polished steel glinting even under the gray sky. Their plate fitted close across their chests, and their helmets left only a slit for the eyes and a gap for the mouth. Heavy spears rode their shoulders. Great shields hung across their backs, lacquered deep blue and marked with a single white tower. Their metal boots struck the cobbles step for step, and the crowd shuffled aside to let them through.

"Mythrandis," Tobin breathed. His hands had stilled on his loaves. "What's the academy want in a place like this?"

Dusty didn't answer.

The elf drifted from stall to stall. She plucked a clay cup from the potter's table, rapped it with a knuckle, and tilted her head to listen before setting it down and rapping another. She crouched before the tinker's rack and ran her fingertips down the dented copper pots. She leaned so close to the fishmonger's scale that he pulled it away from her. Her lips never stopped moving, and when the wind turned Dusty caught pieces of it.

"—no, too thick, the wall's too — and that one's cracked, obviously it would — why does nobody here make anything thin—"

A small hand crept over the edge of the counter.

Dusty had marked the boy an hour ago, six or seven years old and thin as a rake handle, crouched behind the rain barrel working up his nerve. Dusty drew his hand from beneath his cloak and turned his palm up. A flame bloomed there, candle-sized and orange, and around his fingers the air darkened into a black haze traced by a hairline of silver.

The boy's hand froze an inch from the bread. His eyes climbed from the flame to the hood above it.

"Please don't," Dusty said.

The boy bolted, bare feet slapping through the puddles. Dusty let the flame burn a moment longer, then closed his fist around it.

The second was a teen near Dusty's age, broad through the shoulders, a boil swelling red on his neck and a cudgel tucked in his belt. He walked straight to the counter with his chin lifted, took a loaf in each hand, and turned to go.

Dusty covered the distance in three long strides, the flame lit and hovering a finger's width from the teen's left eye. The heat curled his lashes. His pupils shrank to pinpricks.

"Put them back, please."

The teen's hand drifted toward his cudgel. Dusty edged the flame closer and the hand stopped. The teen set the loaves down one at a time, gently, spat into the mud at Dusty's feet, and walked away.

Tobin let out a long breath through his teeth.

The third came as the clouds darkened toward rain. A man, stooped like someone twice Tobin's age, his skin hanging from his skull like wet paper. Sores bloomed across his cheeks and down his throat, weeping yellow at the edges and crusted brown at the centers. A deep, tearing cough shook him with every step, and the crowd drew back wherever he walked. His fever-bright eyes stayed fixed on the bread, and he reached for it with a hand blistered to the wrist.

Tobin stumbled back from the counter, his apron clutched to his mouth.

Dusty's flame was already burning. "Sir. Please stop there."

The man kept reaching.

"Sir. Please."

His fingers closed around the nearest loaf.

Dusty caught his wrist and pressed the flame against the back of his hand.

The skin hissed, and the man screamed. He wrenched free and the loaf tumbled into the gutter, and he fell in after it, sprawling in the muck with his hand clutched to his chest. No one went near him. When he found his feet he limped away sobbing, and the market swallowed the sound.

Dusty closed his fist over the flame and wiped his other hand on his cloak. Fingers, palm, wrist.

Across the square the elf stood beside the apple cart, a bruised apple forgotten in her hand. Her light blue eyes were wide and unblinking, fixed on his closed fist. One of her guards bent to murmur in her ear. She gave no sign of hearing him.

Dusty turned his shoulder to her.

Tobin fished the loaf out of the gutter with two fingers, looked at it, and flung it farther down the gutter.

The cathedral bell tolled noon from the high city, muffled beneath the clouds. Dusty caught Tobin's awning as it came down and folded the damp canvas without being asked. The old man reached beneath the counter and brought up three dark loaves, their crusts gone hard as bark.

"Three today." Tobin pushed them into Dusty's arms and didn't let go right away. "For the sick one. That was ugly work."

"Thank you, sir."

Dusty tucked the loaves inside his rags against his chest, drew his cloak tight over them, and took the southern stair down out of the market, where the cobbles crumbled away into mud.

The Greys sat in the low bowl of Helvanthor where a buried river still soaked up through the ground. Tenements leaned in from both sides of the lane, timbers black with rot, their upper floors nearly touching overhead so the sky was only a thin gray seam. Water dripped from broken gutters and pooled in the ruts, black and still. Refuse had drifted into every corner, rinds and fish bones and rags, and the cold, dank stench of it coated the back of Dusty's throat.

Bodies huddled in the doorways in knots of four and five, bundled in sacking. Old men with rheumy eyes. Mothers with babies tucked into their shawls. Children with sharp faces. Their eyes dropped to the lump beneath his cloak and tracked it down the lane.

Dusty kept to the center of the street, away from the doorways and the mouths of the alleys, his arm crossed tight over the bread, weight balanced on the balls of his feet. His free hand hung open at his side.

Three streets in, they came.

A collapsed tenement slumped on the corner, its beams jutting from the rubble like broken ribs, and they slipped out from beneath it one after another. Ten of them, maybe more, none older than twelve, faces streaked black with soot and feet bare and bleeding. Each small fist held something: a length of rotted wood flaking black, a rock pried from the lane, a clod of mud baked hard.

The boy at the front had a split lip and a missing ear, and his eyes went straight to the bread.

"Give it here."

"I'm sorry," Dusty said. "I can't."

The boy threw his rock.

Dusty turned his head and it cracked across his cheekbone instead of his temple. A clod of dirt burst against his hood, raining grit into his eyes, and the rest of them rushed him from every side, shrieking.

He lit his flame.

A girl came at him screaming with her mouth wide open, and he thrust the flame past her teeth. She gagged and dropped to her knees, clawing at her tongue. A boy swung a board at his head. Dusty ducked under it and swept the flame across the boy's eyes, and the boy reeled away blind, howling. Dusty drove his knee into another's belly, then cracked his elbow across a fourth's nose, and blood sprayed hot across his knuckles.

The rotted wood broke across his back and he staggered. A rock slammed into his thigh. Small hands clawed at his cloak, his arms, his legs, and one of them tore a loaf free from his rags. He spun, but the girl was already gone, darting beneath the fallen beams with it clutched to her chest.

He curled his arm tighter over the two that were left.

They came again. He was taller than any of them by half, longer in the reach, and he had fought in these lanes since before most of them could walk. He hooked an ankle and sent a boy face-first into a black puddle. He caught a wrist and twisted until the rock fell loose. The flame jumped from face to face, licking a cheek, flashing before an eye, darting at an open mouth. Every time one flinched, another hesitated behind it.

Their charge faltered. They spread into a ring around him, weapons raised, feet planted in the mud. The boy with the missing ear stood among them clutching a fresh rock, blood trickling from his nose.

Dusty held the flame up between them.

"Please," he said. "Go home."

The boy dropped his rock into the mud and ran. The rest scattered after him into the alleys and beneath the beams, the blinded ones led by the hands of those who could still see.

Dusty closed his fist and the flame went out. His mana sat steady and full inside him. His lungs burned. He bent with his hands on his knees, breath tearing out of him in ragged white clouds, sweat running cold beneath his rags. His cheek throbbed. His thigh ached, and a hot line burned across his back where the wood had split.

He pressed his arm to his chest. Two loaves.

He straightened and started walking.

"Halt!"

The shout rang off the tenement walls, followed by the clang of steel boots on stone.

Dusty turned.

A Mythrandis guard stood at the mouth of the lane with his spear leveled at Dusty's chest, the white tower on his shield splashed with mud. The second came up behind him with his spear still shouldered, lip curled back from his teeth as he took in the doorways and the black water.

Dusty's shoulders drew tight. His weight dropped to his toes, and his eyes slid past the guards to the alleys behind them, counting the steps to the nearest one.

"No — Holt, stop, stop that, you'll — put it up, he's not going to — why is everything a spear with you?"

The elf stepped out from behind the guard's shoulder, her green cloak dragging through a puddle, cheeks flushed from the chase. She laid a small pale hand flat against the shaft of his spear and pushed it aside like a curtain. The guard, a big man with a heavy jaw, let her.

"And could you — all of them, there, the —" She waved a hand at the doorways, where the huddled bodies had leaned forward to watch. "Can you move them? Back? Just a little, I can't — they're all breathing, it's very loud. Please."

Holt's jaw worked beneath his helmet. He strode to the nearest doorway and rapped the butt of his spear against the frame, and the bodies there scrambled up and away.

The elf walked toward Dusty.

He stepped back.

She kept coming, and he kept retreating until his shoulders met the wet stone of the tenement wall. She stopped close enough that he could smell rain and old paper on her. The top of her head barely reached his chin, and she tipped her face back, her wide blue eyes running over him from hood to boots.

"Hello. I'm Valia. Valia Velanthus." A pink wisp of hair fell across her eyes and she blew it away. "What's yours?"

Dusty said nothing.

"Your name. You do have — well, of course you do, everyone does, I only meant — could I have it?"

Dusty said nothing.

The second guard pushed past Holt, sliding his spear down off his shoulder. He was younger and leaner, with a thin mouth. "The lady asked you a question, street filth. You'll give her an answer, and you'll give it with respect—"

Valia flicked her fingers at him without turning her head. "Not now, Pell. Go stand — over there. With Holt. Go on."

Pell's mouth pressed thin. He went.

"I'm sorry about them. They're very —" She searched for the word and gave up on it. "They're not mine. I mean, I'm not anyone. Not a lady, whatever Pell says, not a noble or a magistrate, nothing that — I'm a researcher. At Mythrandis. I study mana, the — the how of it, the why, where it goes when it goes, that sort of — and they only come along so I don't get stabbed. Which I apparently would. Constantly. According to them." She tipped her head back up the lane, toward the market. "It's the same as you, this morning. With the bread man. You were guarding him. They're guarding me. It's exactly the same thing."

Dusty cast his gaze past her to the polished steel, the lacquered shields, Pell's thin mouth. He thought of Tobin's floury hands pressing the loaves into his arms. His jaw loosened.

"Dusty," he said.

"Dusty." She said it again under her breath, as if writing it down. "Dusty. Good. Dusty, your hand. This morning at the stall, and just now, with the — all of those children, I watched from the —" She waved behind her. "Your fire was so small."

"Yes, ma'am. That's all there is."

"No, no, small isn't the — small doesn't matter, I don't care about small, most people are small, it's the color." Her hands came up between them, fingers spread and curling as though she held the flame herself. "Black. Around your fingers. And the edge of it, the very edge — silver, wasn't it? It was silver. Thin as a hair. Silver."

Dusty glanced down at his hand. The dark haze had clung to his fingers for as long as he could remember.

"Is that wrong, ma'am?"

"Wrong? I don't — I don't know. I don't know what it is. That's what's so —" She caught his wrist in both hands and turned it palm up, bending over the grime of his fingers. Her grip was warm, and stronger than it looked. "When did it start? Did someone teach you? When you draw on it, does it feel shallow, or deep, or — is there a bottom, can you feel where it —"

Dusty drew his hand out of hers. "I'm sorry. I don't know how to answer that."

"Have you ever run out?"

"No, ma'am."

"Never?" Her eyes went wider. "You lit it — I counted, I counted twenty-three times just now, and you're not — you don't even feel it? Not a little?"

"No, ma'am."

"But it's so small. It should have been gone after — a flame that size, from a well that size, I'd give it ten minutes, fifteen if you were — how long can you hold it? The longest?"

Cold nights came back to him. A small back pressed against his chest, the black glow cupped in his palm until gray light crept in from the east.

"All night, ma'am."

Valia opened her mouth. She closed it. She opened it again. "That's — that isn't — that's not how any of it —" She seized the end of her braid and twisted it hard in her fist. "Why? What does the silver do? Does it feel different at the edge than the middle? Is it warmer, is it colder, did your mother have it, your father, does it change when you're —"

"I'm sorry. I don't know."

"You don't know. You don't — of course you don't, why would you, who would have ever — gods, it's like having the answer right in front of me in a language I can't —" A sound somewhere between a groan and a growl escaped her, and her hands dropped to her sides. "I can't do anything here. I haven't got any of my — not the stones, not the — I'd need the lab. I'd need weeks. Months."

Her gaze drifted to the dripping stone beside his head. Her lips kept moving, softer now, and the words weren't for him.

"Months, then. Months is fine if he's — if he's close. If he's on the island. The Headmaster could — well, he always says yes, he's never once said no, so there's no reason — and there's the lottery, there's always a spot left over in the lottery, isn't there, there's always — yes. And he'd need classes anyway, he can't just sit in my lab, the Maids would never — but that's fine, that's better, actually, I'd see how it changes as he — yes." She nodded to herself, sharp. "Yes. Good."

Her eyes snapped back to his.

"Dusty. How would you like to go to a magic school?"

Dusty stared down at her.

Water dripped from the eaves. Back by the collapsed tenement, one of the children was still crying. Holt had turned from the doorways, his spear forgotten against his shoulder, and Pell's thin mouth hung open.

Dusty had answers ready for the watch when they came asking questions, for the priests when they came with their thin soup, for the men who sometimes came looking for boys who could fight. His mouth stayed shut.

"The academy," Valia pressed on, as though he had answered. "Mythrandis. You'd have a room. You'd have meals, three of them, every day, and tea, the Maids do tea, and classes, and I'd be — well, I'd be there, I'd be studying you, but only a little, only the — I'd help. With whatever it is." She gestured at his hand. "Whatever that is."

"Thank you, ma'am," Dusty said finally. "But I can't. I have other responsibilities."

"Oh, that's all right." She waved it away. "I'll just go and speak with your parents. Parents always listen once somebody explains about the — where do they live? Is it near?"

"The city guard comes through the Greys when the coughing sickness spreads," Dusty said, as evenly as he might quote the price of rye. "They close off the worst streets. Then they burn them out, with whoever is still inside." He paused. "My parents were inside. Eight years ago."

Valia went still. Her hands hung frozen in the air, her lips parted around whatever word had been next, her wide blue eyes emptied for half a breath.

Then the color drained from her cheeks. "They — the city? The crown's own — with people still — and nobody —" She turned to stare at Holt and Pell. Pell looked at his boots. She spun back to Dusty. "Then you're coming. No. You are. You can't stay here, not in a place that — you can't."

"I'm sorry, ma'am. I can't go."

"Why not?" She flung her arm out at the lane, the rotting walls, the black pools, the huddled bodies in the doorways. "What is so important that you'd stay in this? What could possibly be worth — I'm offering you a way out. An actual way out, a real one, and you'd rather have —"

"My sister is sick."

Valia's mouth closed.

"She's twelve," Dusty said. "She's been sick a long time. Nobody knows what it is. I'm all she has." He met her eyes. "I won't leave her."

Her arm lowered to her side. Her fingers loosened from the braid, and her gaze moved over him: the filthy hood, the blood drying brown on his knuckles, the arm still pressed across two stale loaves of bread. Her full lips parted, then closed again.

"A sister," she said at last.

"Yes, ma'am."

"What's her name?"

Dusty hesitated. "Rinnie."

"Rinnie." One corner of Valia's mouth lifted. Then the smile spread, and her hands began moving again. "Well, but that's — that's simple, then. That's not a reason at all. There's a city at the end of the bridge. Aethres. On the mainland side, right at the foot of it, you can see the towers from its market. I could find her a room. I'm almost sure I could find her a room. And you could visit her, on rest days, between studies, it's no distance at all, the Guard walks students across all the time—"

Dusty's eyes narrowed. He turned the offer over and ran his thumb along its seams, feeling for the hook. In the Greys, nothing came without a hook.

Valia chattered on, heedless of his eyes. "And the healers. Mythrandis has healers. The best, truly the best anywhere, and I know the Headmaster, and he'd — I could have some of them look at her. They'd find what it is. They find what everything is."

Dusty's gaze slid past her shoulder.

Holt stood at the mouth of the lane with his heavy jaw set, nose wrinkled beneath the rim of his helmet. Beside him Pell stared at Dusty the way a man stares at a dead rat in his path, lip curled, eyes hard. Dusty stared back and pictured an island full of Pells, spears and lacquered shields, lordlings in fine blazers, men who stood at the mouths of burning streets and made sure nobody came out.

Valia glanced back at the guards. When she turned around again her wide eyes had narrowed and settled on his.

"You could learn it yourself," she said.

Dusty's eyes came back to her.

"Healing. It's one of the three disciplines, and you — Dusty, you'd be —" She stepped closer, and this time he held his ground. "Healing isn't about how much mana you have. Everyone thinks it is, but it isn't. It's about how long you can hold it steady. How long you can keep pouring without spilling, without stopping. And you never stop." Her voice dropped low, each word slow. "You could heal her. Yourself. With your own two hands."

Water dripped from a broken gutter. Far off, the cathedral bell tolled the half hour.

Dusty's thoughts drifted to Rinnie's hands trembling beneath her thin blanket, and how she tucked them away whenever she heard him at the door. He thought of the smile she fixed onto her face each time he stepped inside, and the black glow in his palm pressed to her back through all those cold nights, doing nothing more than keeping her warm.

"I'll come," he said.

Valia's smile split her face, crinkling the corners of her blue eyes, bright as a lamp lit in a cellar. She spun on her heel and clapped her hands, the sharp crack of it bouncing off the dripping walls.

"Holt! Pell! There'll be — we're taking more back with us. Two more." She beamed at them. "New travelers!"

Holt and Pell looked at one another through the slits of their helmets. Neither said a word.

Valia turned back to Dusty and folded her hands before her cloak. Her fingers were still, her feet planted, the wisps of pink hair settling against her cheeks.

"Would you take me to see her? Please?"

Dusty pushed off the wall, shifted the two loaves against his chest, and drew his hood lower over his face.

"Will her room have a window?" he asked. "A window for the sun?"

***

The clouds had thinned over the Devout Quarter, and a pale, watery light silvered the wet flagstones of the courtyard. Beyond the rooftops the cathedral rose in tiers of white stone, its spires climbing so tall and thin they scratched at the gray underbellies of the clouds, gold leaf gone dull at every tip. Bronze bells hung silent in the nearest tower, streaked green with age.

Priests drifted across the courtyard in twos and threes, their cream robes brushing the stones without a fleck of mud. Censers swung from silver chains in their hands, trailing thin ribbons of smoke that smelled of cedar and honey. Their eyes passed over Dusty and kept going.

He tugged at his collar. Brown linen, plain and new, stiff at the neck and stiff at the knees, and boots with soles that did not let the wet through. Valia had pulled the first tailor in the city out of bed that morning, chattering about seams while the man measured, and afterward Dusty had stood in the tailor's back room over a bucket of steaming water and scrubbed until his skin burned pink.

Rinnie stirred against his chest.

She weighed next to nothing. He carried her the way he always had, one arm beneath her knees and the other behind her back, her head tucked into the hollow of his shoulder. Valia had bought her a gown of soft white cotton and a thick black cloak whose heavy wool swallowed her to the chin. Her face peered out from the deep hood, small and pale, her black hair tied back with the frayed blue ribbon he had hauled crates for two days to buy her the winter she turned eight. Bruised shadows cupped her black eyes. Her lips were the color of milk.

Her eyes roamed everything.

"There's a girl," she said.

Dusty followed her gaze across the courtyard.

Eight armed men marched in a hollow square, breastplates of white enamel trimmed in gold, long swords riding their hips, their faces hard beneath open helms. At the center walked a girl no older than Rinnie. White silk fell to her ankles, and her hair, a gold so pale it was nearly white, hung loose down her back. Her hands were folded before her, her eyes fixed on the stones.

A priest bent nearly double with age led her by the wrist, his spine curved like a shepherd's crook, a staff of black wood tapping ahead of him. His spotted fingers never loosened. She matched his shuffle step for small step and never lifted her head.

The priests in the courtyard stopped as she passed, bowing, some touching two fingers to their lips.

The soup priests in the Greys had spoken of saintesses, girls born with the gods' own light in them. Dusty watched the old man's grip on her thin wrist, the ring of swords around her, her lowered eyes.

"She looks tired," Rinnie said.

The procession passed beneath a stone archway and was gone.

"This way, this way, it's just — here." Valia swept ahead with her green cloak flaring behind her, Holt and Pell clanking at her heels. "It's the big one. The one with no — you'll see."

The building filled the western edge of the courtyard, wide and tall, white marble veined in gray, its walls climbing smooth and windowless to a great dome. A pair of bronze doors taller than three men stood between columns thick as old oaks. Guards in the same white enamel as the saintess's escort flanked them, spears grounded.

The nearest stepped forward and held out a gauntleted hand. "Papers."

"Yes. Of course. Papers, I have — they're here." Valia patted the front of her cloak. She dug into a pocket and came out with a pencil stub and a scrap of cramped writing, and put them back. The next pocket gave up a copper wire bent into a spiral, a dried leaf, and a small brass bell with no clapper. She burrowed into a pocket inside her cloak, then a pocket inside that one. "They were — I had them this morning, I know I had them, Holt, did I give them to —"

"Left side, my lady," Holt said. "Inside."

"Left side. Inside." She found them and beamed at Holt, pressing a thick fold of parchment into the guard's hand, its red wax stamped with the white tower.

The guard read it twice. His eyes flicked to Dusty, to Rinnie, and back to the seal. He handed it over and stepped aside, and the bronze doors swung open without a sound.

The dome swallowed them.

Polished white stone spread in a vast circle beneath a ceiling painted deep blue and scattered with gold stars. At its center, floating above a raised platform of black marble, hung the crystal.

It was the size of three horses standing nose to tail. Its facets were cut clean and sharp, and they caught the lamplight and flung it across the dome, freckling the walls and floor and painted stars with trembling points of white and blue and violet. No chain held it. No frame. It turned so slowly that Dusty watched a long while before he was sure it moved.

Four mages in gray robes stood at the platform's edges with their hands raised, light glowing yellow and green and blue around their fingers, lips moving in unison. A little way back waited three men and two women in velvet and fur, rings flashing on every finger, servants standing behind them over a stack of leather trunks. One of the women held a lace handkerchief pressed to her nose. Her eyes found Dusty and Rinnie and narrowed above the lace.

Dusty's arms tightened around his sister.

The mages lowered their hands. A priest in cream robes led the nobles up three black steps onto the platform, the servants following with the trunks, and they gathered beneath the crystal in a loose knot, the handkerchief still at the woman's face.

The crystal pulsed.

White light swelled from its heart and filled every facet at once, and a note rang out of it, low and clear, a great bell struck once. It hummed up through the stone into the soles of Dusty's boots and set his teeth buzzing. The freckles of light on the walls flared and stretched into long bright streaks. Light poured from the crystal onto the platform like water from an overturned basin, and the nobles vanished inside it.

The light drained away. The note faded. The platform stood empty, black marble gleaming.

Rinnie's fingers had knotted in the collar of his shirt. Her eyes were wide and black, the white light still caught in them.

"Where did they go?" she whispered.

"Oh, the — they went where they were going. Calvenne, I think, that was their — the mages said Calvenne." Valia started across the floor toward the platform, and Dusty followed, Holt and Pell falling in behind. "It's a Way Station. The Church builds them. Runs them. Maintains them, I suppose, that's the — it sends you. Far. Very far, very fast, all at once. That's how we'll get to Aethres, it's much too far to walk, and the roads are — well, the roads are very long."

Rinnie studied the crystal as they drew nearer, its colors sliding across her pale face.

"Did they borrow it from a giant?"

Behind them, Pell snorted.

Dusty turned his head and fixed his black eyes on the guard. He held them there until Pell's thin mouth closed and his gaze slid away to the painted stars.

Valia laughed, bright and delighted, and dropped back to walk beside Rinnie. "No, no giants. Though that would be — no. They grow them. Mages do. They take a seed stone, a tiny one, no bigger than your thumbnail, and they pour mana into it. Every day, in a very particular way, the same way, for years. Decades. One mage, then another when that one gets too old, then another. It grows a little every time." Her hands shaped the crystal in the air, wider and wider. "And when it's big enough, they give it to a Way Station. But it only has so many uses in it. Every time it sends someone it wears a little, and one day it cracks, right through, and then they have to bring in a new one and start growing another. It's terribly expensive. Terribly. I've always wanted to put my ear against one while it —"

"Next," called one of the mages.

They climbed the three black steps. Cold rose from the marble through the soles of Dusty's new boots. Holt and Pell took their places at the edges, spears grounded. Above, the crystal turned, and Dusty looked up into it and found his own face scattered across a hundred facets, pale and hollow-cheeked.

Rinnie squeaked.

His arms had locked around her, fingers digging into the black wool, crushing her thin body against his ribs. He loosened them at once.

"Sorry. I'm sorry, Rinnie. Did I hurt you?"

"No." She smiled up at him, small and thin, the corners of it held in place. "You're squishing the cloak. It's new."

The mages raised their hands, light gathering yellow and green and blue around their fingers.

Valia stepped in close and laid one hand on Rinnie's shoulder and the other on Dusty's.

"It's safe. It is. Very safe. I've done it — I don't know, thirty times? Forty? You feel a sort of — well, you don't feel anything, really, it's like a blink, except longer, except it isn't. And nobody's ever — I mean, I've never heard of anybody — it's safe." She gave Rinnie's shoulder a small squeeze. "You'll like it. It sounds lovely from the inside."

Her hand left Rinnie. The one on Dusty's shoulder stayed.

She was looking up at him, her wide blue eyes searching his face, her full lips parted around words that never came. Her fingers pressed once through the linen, warm against his shoulder.

She smiled, let go, and stepped back to stand beside him.

The crystal pulsed. The bell rang out deep and clear and shook through Dusty's chest. Rinnie pressed her face into his neck, and he held her as tightly as he dared as the white light poured down over them.

***

Amber light struck Dusty's face as the bronze doors swung open, and he squinted against it. A breath ago gray clouds had smothered Helvanthor. Now the sky stretched wide and clear above him, the sun sinking low over the rooftops and pouring gold down the street, thin clouds burning peach and rose along their edges. The warm air carried the smell of baking bread and horses and hot stone cooling after a long day.

Rinnie lifted her face from his neck, blinking into the glare. "It's evening," she said. "It was morning."

"I know." Dusty shifted her higher against his chest. "I saw."

Smooth gray paving ran beneath his boots, laid tight without a rut or puddle in it. Buildings of honey-colored brick and dark timber lined the street, flower boxes spilling red and yellow from their windows. Past the last rooftops green fields rolled away toward the hills. It was a town, but a large one, clean and rich. Behind him the Way Station's marble dome blushed pink in the sunset, a smaller cathedral beside it lifting a single gold-capped spire. At the far end of town a sprawling manor of pale stone crowned a rise, its many windows burning orange, a blue banner snapping from its highest tower.

"The Duke's," Valia said, following his gaze, already walking. "He runs the — well, everything, here. This way. We have to find — they should be — this way."

She led them down the steps into a broad market square crowded with stalls beneath striped canvas awnings of blue and white and saffron. People drifted between them with the slow, easy stride of folk whose work was done. Sausages spat over a charcoal grill, fat smoke rolling across the square, and somewhere a fiddle scraped out a jig while a knot of girls clapped along. By a flower stall a handful of town guards lounged with helmets tucked under their arms and collars loosened. The girl behind the buckets of blooms was young and freckled, a sprig of lavender tucked behind her ear, and she leaned over the counter to swat one of them with a daisy. He clutched his chest as though run through, and his companions roared.

Dusty's eyes swept over hands, pockets, alley mouths. Nobody here was starving. Nobody watched him. His shoulders stayed drawn tight.

Valia stopped in the center of the square and turned a slow circle, her braid swinging, wide blue eyes skipping from stall to stall and face to face. She rose onto her toes and turned the other way. Holt and Pell planted themselves at her back, scanning over her head. "They're — they should be here. They said they'd be — I told them the Way Station, I know I told them the —"

"Good evening, Lady Valia."

The voice came from just beside Dusty's elbow. He spun, and found two women standing inside their circle where a heartbeat ago there had been only paving stones and sunset. No footstep. No shadow. Holt jerked back a full pace with his spear half raised, and Pell cursed and nearly dropped his. Valia let out a high yelp and clapped both hands over her mouth. Dusty's weight dropped to his toes, and he turned his shoulder between the strangers and Rinnie, his arms locking around her.

They wore black dresses buttoned snug to the throat, long sleeves ending in black lace gloves, white aprons crisp and spotless over skirts that stopped just above the knee. White stockings ran down to black heels so thin and tall that Dusty could not fathom how either had crossed the stones without a sound.

The shorter one was smiling. Light brown hair swept up into a high ponytail that fell to the small of her back, swaying as she tilted her head, and the ties of her apron cinched a narrow waist between full hips and a fuller chest. Her hazel eyes caught the sunset in flecks of green and gold, and a blush of rose was painted on her cheeks and lips.

The other stood a head taller, willowy and straight-backed, her hips slim beneath the apron and her chest heavy against the black bodice. Dark blue hair fell straight and glossy past her shoulders without a strand astray. Eyes of the same deep blue, lined sharp in kohl, took in Dusty, Rinnie, the guards, and the market behind them in one unhurried sweep.

Rinnie laughed, a thin, breathy sound Dusty had not heard in weeks. "How did you do that?"

The shorter woman's smile widened. She bent at the waist until her hazel eyes met Rinnie's black ones and laid one lace-gloved finger across her painted lips. Rinnie pressed a finger to her own lips in answer, and the woman's eyes crinkled.

"Gwenith. Maedelyn." Valia lowered her hands, her cheeks flushed pink to the tips of her pointed ears. "You — must you always — every time, you do that every single time —"

"Yes, Lady Valia," said the tall one, folding her gloved hands before her apron. "We have reserved three rooms at the Gilded Pheasant, two streets east. The supplies for the journey to the academy have been collected and packed. The horses are stabled. We depart at first light."

"Oh. Good. That's — good, that's very —" Valia nodded, and kept nodding. "Thank you."

Dusty's grip had not loosened. "Pardon me, ma'am." Both maids turned to him. "My sister can't ride," he said. "What arrangements are there for her?"

Valia's mouth fell open. Her eyes dropped to Rinnie, small and colorless inside the black cloak, and the flush drained from her face before flooding back crimson. "I — oh. Oh, no. I didn't — I never even — a cart, or a — how did I not — she can't sit a horse for four days, of course she can't, she can barely —" She seized the end of her braid and wrung it between her fists. "Maedelyn, it wasn't — I didn't plan any of this, it was only supposed to be the potters' stalls, I was looking at the glazes, for the resonance, you know, the thin ones, and then there was this bread stall and he was guarding it, and he did this thing with his hands, this fire, and it was so small, but the color of it was —"

"She can ride with me," Dusty said.

Valia's mouth snapped shut.

"I'll carry her." He kept his voice level and polite, his black eyes on Maedelyn. "I've carried her farther than that. It's no trouble."

Maedelyn's blue gaze rested on him, then drifted down to settle on his hands.

Beside her Gwenith's smile had faded. Her eyes moved from Rinnie's milk-pale lips to the bruised hollows beneath her eyes to the thin wrist peeking from the black wool, and her brows knit together. She drew breath to speak.

"That won't be necessary," Maedelyn said, and Gwenith closed her mouth. "We observed your party leaving the Way Station," Maedelyn went on. "We made certain assumptions. An additional horse has been secured for you, young man. And a small covered cart with a pony to draw it, for the young lady. It has cushions and a roof." She paused. "And side panels that open, should she wish to look out."

Dusty stood silent while the fiddle sawed on behind him. Then he bent from the waist, as low as he could with Rinnie cradled against him. "Thank you," he said. "Thank you very much."

Rinnie wriggled in his arms, tucking her chin as far as the heavy hood allowed, her small hands folded atop the black wool. "Thank you," she said. "I like looking out."

Gwenith made a sound like a kettle on the edge of the boil and pressed both gloved hands to her cheeks. "Oh, look at them. Look at them, Maedelyn. They bowed. Both of them. She bowed from inside his arms." Her ponytail whipped around as she turned to her companion, hazel eyes shining. "Can we keep them? Please say we can keep them."

Maedelyn closed her eyes and let out a long breath through her nose. "Lady Valia. Guardsmen. Young man. Young lady." She opened her eyes and turned on one needle-thin heel. "This way, please. Supper is waiting, and the rooms will not remain warm on their own."

She set off across the square without a backward glance, the crowd opening before her black skirts. Gwenith fell in at Dusty's side, beaming, and reached up to tug Rinnie's hood straight. Valia hurried after them with her braid bouncing, still apologizing to no one, and Holt and Pell clanked along at the rear, their eyes never leaving the two women in black.

***

The private room of the Gilded Pheasant smelled of woodsmoke and roast fowl, rosemary and hot butter, and Valia could not stop listening to it. The fire popped in the hearth. Knives scraped over pewter. The old floorboards groaned beneath every shifting chair, a low, resonant creak, and she caught herself tapping her fork against the rim of her plate to match its pitch. Close. The oak must be very old and very dry, and if she could only take a proper measurement of the—

Holt cleared his throat. She set the fork down.

Lamplight pooled gold across the long oak table, glistening on the glazed skin of two roasted pheasants, a steaming crock of barley stew thick with carrots, a heaped board of dark bread, and a wheel of yellow cheese already missing a generous wedge on Holt's side. Maedelyn stood beside the door with her gloved hands folded over her apron, straight as a candle, her blue eyes moving slowly down the table and back. She had refused a chair twice.

At the far end Rinnie sat nested in a cushioned chair Gwenith had dragged in from the common room, a blanket over her knees and the black cloak draped behind her narrow shoulders. Gwenith knelt at her side, humming as she cut pheasant into pieces smaller than Valia's thumbnail.

Dusty sat beside his sister, his own bowl going cold. The spoon had risen halfway to his mouth three times and drifted back down three times. His black eyes stayed fixed on Rinnie's plate, his jaw loosening each time she swallowed and tightening each time she set her fork down. "One more," he said. "Please, Rinnie. Just one more."

"I've had four."

"You can have more than four. You don't have to ration right now." His voice stayed low, but it had hardened. "There's enough. There's a whole second bird. Eat."

Rinnie's pale lower lip pushed out. "I have to eat slow. You know I have to eat slow, or it all comes back up." She nudged his bowl toward him with one thin finger. "Eat yours. It's getting cold."

"I'm fine." He waved it off without a glance and slid another sliver of pheasant from Gwenith's board onto her plate. "Try the carrots, then. They're soft."

Across the table Holt caught Pell's eye. Pell shook his head slowly, his thin mouth twisting as he tore a leg from the pheasant, and Holt huffed through his nose.

Valia's fingers curled against the oak. She had seen that look on them in the Greys, when Dusty stood in the mud with blood drying on his knuckles, and again at the Way Station. Now he sat scrubbed pink in clean brown linen, and still their eyes slid over him the same way, for sitting at a table, for wanting his sister to eat.

She cleared her throat, louder than she intended, and every head turned. "Dusty." She sat up straighter. "The habits you — the ones from the capital, from the Greys, the rationing and the — those won't serve you. Not here. Not where you're going." Good. That sounded like a professor. She pressed on. "You should be eating at least as much as she is. More. You're — look at you, you're so much thinner than she is, and she's the one who's — and you're going to have to get used to other people looking after her, because you'll be in classes, and there'll be healers, and they know what they're doing, mostly, and you can't be there every — you can't hover over her every minute, you'll wear yourself — I only mean that you should — please just eat something."

Her voice had shrunk to almost nothing by the end.

Dusty sat very still, the spoon resting in his long fingers, his black eyes on her face. The fire crackled. Heat climbed Valia's cheeks. She was no teacher. She measured the tremble of copper wire and filled notebooks no one opened, and she had just lectured a boy who had kept his sister alive eight years in a slum on how to care for her. She opened her mouth to take it back.

Rinnie laid her small hand over his, pale and bird-boned against his long fingers, the knuckles standing out sharp. "Dusty," she said softly. "Things are different now. Please start taking care of yourself too. For me."

His hand stayed still beneath hers. His throat worked.

Gwenith laughed, warm and bright, and plucked the spoon from Rinnie's plate to wave it at him. "Go on, then, big brother. I'll watch her eat every single bite. I'll count them out loud if you like. One, two, three, all the way to the bottom of the bowl."

Dusty looked at his bowl, then at Rinnie. His knuckles whitened around the spoon, and it did not rise.

Valia coughed. She laid both palms flat on the table, and she looked him straight in the eyes. "Your sister is in better hands right now than yours. You look like a skeleton. Eat."

Holt stopped chewing. Across the room one of Maedelyn's dark blue brows lifted a hair's breadth.

Valia held Dusty's gaze. His pupils swallowed the lamplight until his eyes were black pools. A log shifted in the grate, spilling sparks. Her own eyes began to sting, and she did not blink.

The corner of his mouth lifted, crooked and brief. He bent his head over the bowl and began to eat.

He took the stew in long, fast spoonfuls, then tore a heel from the bread and scraped the bowl clean. Gwenith, grinning, slid a plate of pheasant before him, and he stripped the meat from the bones with his fingers. His head stayed bowed low over the table, and in the poor light Valia thought she saw his eyes shining wet before he dragged his sleeve across them.

She looked down at her plate, a smile tugging at her lips.

He ate four plates, then a fifth, slower, while Holt and Pell gaped, Gwenith applauded each empty one, and Rinnie counted them aloud in a flat, pleased little voice. When he finally sat back with a hand pressed to his stomach, bones and crusts littered the table between him and his sister.

When the fire burned low and the men rose to retire across the hall, Dusty lingered at Rinnie's chair. He tucked the blanket higher over her knees and smoothed a black strand back beneath her ribbon, then crouched to search her face. "Are you warm enough? If you wake up and you're cold—"

"Dusty." Rinnie aimed one small, imperious finger at the door. "Go to bed."

"I only want to—"

"Bed."

He rose, slowly. At the doorway his eyes found Valia's across the room, and he dipped his head to her, low and unhurried. Then he was gone, and she sat listening to his footsteps creak across the hall.

The women's room was small and warm, three narrow beds and a cot tucked beneath a sloping ceiling, a single lamp burning on the washstand. Valia sat cross-legged on her bed and worked her fingers through the pink tangles of her unbound braid. Her father's old brown robe lay folded at the foot of the mattress, elbows patched, hem worn thin. She pulled at a knot and did not look at it.

Behind Rinnie on the cot, Gwenith drew a brush through the girl's black hair in long, careful strokes. The frayed blue ribbon lay in Rinnie's lap, her fingers tracing its worn edges. "How do you become a maid?" Rinnie asked.

The brush paused. "Oh, you know. Lots of ways."

"What ways?"

"Well." The brush started again. "Some are asked. Some ask."

"Do you train a long time?"

"Ages." Gwenith smiled, her hazel eyes drifting to the lamp. "Just ages."

"Doing what?"

"This and that. Cleaning. Cooking. Tea." Stroke. "Very important tea."

Rinnie tilted her head into the brush. "You didn't walk on the stones. In the market. Your shoes didn't make any noise."

Gwenith's lips parted and closed. Her glance slid toward the door, where Maedelyn sat straight-backed on the edge of her bed, drawing pins from her dark blue hair.

"Can I become one?" Rinnie asked.

Gwenith's brush hovered. Her brows drew up and together, her lips parted around a yes she did not say.

"All actions must be with Beauty, Elegance, and Dignity above all else," Maedelyn said.

Valia's fingers stilled in her hair.

Maedelyn laid her pins in a neat silver row on the washstand. "Long ago, the Maids served a monster. When he died, we chose to serve the academy instead. We have served it ever since." She drew the last pin, and her dark blue hair fell straight and glossy down her back. "That is all there is to say."

In forty years of Maids trading places in her lab, Valia had never heard one of them say so much. A monster? Which monster? She opened her mouth and found Maedelyn's calm blue eyes waiting on her, and she shut it again.

Rinnie frowned down at the ribbon, turning it over between her fingers. "That's not very much," she said. She did not ask again. Her black eyes lifted and found Valia instead, and Valia sat caught in them like a moth against the lamp glass.

"What do you research?"

"Oh! Sound." Valia sat up, her hands already moving. "Well, mana first, but mana and sound, whether you can — you know how a bell rings, when you strike it, it shakes the air, and the air shakes your ear, and — I want to know if mana can shake the air. On its own. Without the bell. So you'd hear it."

"Like the crystal," Rinnie said. "It rang."

Valia stared at her. "Yes. Yes, exactly like the — that's exactly — nobody ever gets that."

"How long have you been doing it?"

"Forty years. About. Forty."

Rinnie weighed that. "Do you like it?"

"I—" Valia's hands slowed. "Yes. I think so. Yes."

"Do you have a boyfriend?"

Valia's hands dropped into her lap. Heat crawled up her throat to the tips of her ears. Behind Rinnie, Gwenith made a small choking noise and bent very low over the brush.

"I — no. No. I haven't — there hasn't been much time for — looking. For a — for someone." She cleared her throat. "I'm very busy."

"Do you like the school?"

"Yes. It's beautiful. The towers, and the lake, the light on the water in the mornings, and the library has a ceiling that — well, you'll see."

"Do you have a lot of friends there?" Rinnie tilted her head. "You seem like you'd have a lot of friends."

Valia opened her mouth, and nothing came. Her lab in the east wing rose up before her, long tables buried in notebooks, copper wire strung taut between iron posts. The dining hall, the end seat by the window where the chairs beside her always stood empty. Faculty meetings full of polite nods and "we'll look into it." Her lab door clicking shut behind her every night. Her fingers found the worn patch on the elbow of the brown robe and rubbed at it.

"Not many," she said, quietly. "I don't — I mostly keep to myself. There's a lot of work."

"Lady Valia's research is extremely important," Maedelyn said, smoothing her blanket flat with one gloved hand. "It requires her full attention. Most of the faculty could not keep pace with her if they tried."

Valia looked at her. Maedelyn kept her eyes on the blanket.

Rinnie's eyelids had begun to droop, the bruised shadows beneath them deepening in the lamplight. Gwenith set the brush aside and wove her black hair into a loose braid for sleep. "You shouldn't forget about Dusty, though," Rinnie said. "When you're busy."

"Dusty?"

"You're his first friend."

Valia's hands went still in her lap. "Why do you say that?"

"Because he never had time for one." Rinnie's words had slowed, soft and drifting. "He spent everything on me. All his time. All his money. He'd go without eating so I could. He'd stay up all night so I wouldn't get cold. He watched me every minute, so I wouldn't die." Her small fingers closed around the ribbon. "I'm glad I don't have to be that anymore. Something he has to carry."

The lamp hissed on the washstand. Valia's throat closed tight and aching, her eyes burning. She pressed her lips together and stared at the grain of the floorboards until they swam.

"Will you look after him?" Rinnie asked. "At the school?"

Valia swallowed, and it hurt. "Yes." Her voice cracked, and she tried again. "Yes. I'll make sure he eats. Every meal. And sleeps. Plenty of sleep. I'll — yes. I promise."

Rinnie's lips curved in a small, sleepy smile as Gwenith eased her down onto the pillow, her eyes already closed.

Valia lay back and drew the blanket to her chin. Maedelyn turned down the lamp to a dim orange ember, and the beds creaked as the two maids settled beneath their covers.

The whispering began almost at once, too soft for words, a fierce hiss of consonants flicking back and forth across the dark like the spit of a dying wick. Gwenith's voice rose, pleading. Maedelyn's cut it down, clipped and cold. Then, sharp enough to carry:

"—ask permission first."

Silence.

Valia stared up at the shadowed rafters. Permission for what? From whom? She turned the question over until it slipped from her fingers, the way everything did at the end of a long day. Rinnie's breathing had gone slow and even. Below them the common room fire had burned down to embers, and she could hear the grate ticking as it cooled, a tiny, rhythmic note like a bell struck very far away.

She fell asleep before she could decide what key it was in.

***

Dust hung over the road in a pale golden haze, churned up by hooves and wheels and drifting across fields of ripening wheat. The sun beat down from a cloudless sky, and sweat crept beneath the collar of Dusty's linen shirt and down the length of his spine. The saddle leather creaked with every plodding step of the brown mare beneath him. He had never sat a horse before this morning. His thighs burned, and he clutched the reins in both fists, the leather gone slick in his palms.

Ahead, the little cart rattled along behind a shaggy gray pony. Its canvas roof threw a square of cool shade across the cushions, its sides open to the fields, and through them Dusty watched Rinnie sleep, curled on her side with her head in Gwenith's lap. Her black hair spilled across the white apron, her small chest rising slow and shallow. Gwenith's lace-gloved fingers drifted through the strands, and now and then she glanced back and gave him a small nod.

Valia's horse fell into step beside his. Dust had dulled her green cloak to sage, and loose pink strands clung to her damp temples. Her wide blue eyes flicked to the cart and back to him. "She's been asleep since — she hasn't woken once, has she? Since we left the inn. Is that — should she be —"

"She's all right." Dusty's eyes stayed on the rise and fall of Rinnie's shoulder. "Yesterday was a lot for her. The Way Station, the market, dinner. Staying up with all of you." He paused. "She spent everything she had. Today she pays for it."

Valia nodded slowly, worrying her full lower lip between her teeth.

The cart lurched over a rut. Rinnie did not stir, but Dusty's knuckles went white around the reins, and he nudged the mare clumsily closer to Valia's. "Can I ask you something?"

"Anything. Of course. Ask."

"Why didn't the Way Station send us closer?" He cast his gaze past the cart to the long pale ribbon of road unspooling over the hills. "Maedelyn said four days. It could send us across the world in a breath. Why not all the way?"

Behind them one of the guards snorted, loud and wet inside his helmet.

Valia did not turn. She lifted her chin and spoke to the empty air above the wheat. "Students come to Mythrandis to learn things they don't know. That's the entire point of the place. Nobody should be made to feel ashamed for asking."

Dusty looked back over his shoulder. Pell rode a length behind, his thin mouth curled beneath the rim of his helmet. Dusty held his eyes. "Guards should spend more time guarding," he said pleasantly, "and less time sharing their opinions."

Red flooded Pell's face. He hauled on his reins, and he and Holt wheeled their horses and spurred forward, spears swinging down off their shoulders.

Maedelyn's black mare cut between them. A moment before she had been riding at the head of the column. Now she sat squarely across the road, her horse broadside to the guards, gloved hands resting loose atop the reins, dust swirling around the black hem of her skirt. In the cart, Gwenith had eased Rinnie's head onto the cushions and risen to her feet, standing straight in the swaying bed with her hands folded over her apron. The dimples had vanished from her face. Her hazel eyes were fixed on the guards, and she did not blink.

"Guardsmen." Maedelyn's voice was quiet, and cold as well water. "Your duty is the protection of the academy's students. It is not their correction." Her blue eyes slid from Holt to Pell and stayed there. "I would remind you that the protection of students is the duty of the Maid Corps as well. From any threat."

Wind hissed through the wheat.

Holt's spear came up first. Pell's followed a breath later, the red draining from his face until it matched the pale dust on his plate. Without a word they reined around and fell back, twenty paces, then thirty, until they trailed well behind the column with their eyes fixed on the road.

Dusty watched them go. Pell's hands trembled on his reins. Holt, twice Maedelyn's width in steel, kept his helmet turned from her back. Dusty filed it away beside the silent shoes in the market square.

Gwenith settled back into the cart and drew Rinnie's head into her lap. When she caught Dusty watching, her dimples returned and she wiggled her gloved fingers at him.

Beside him Valia's hands had knotted in her horse's mane, her face gone pale beneath the dust.

Maedelyn brought her mare alongside and laid one gloved hand over Valia's white knuckles. "It is finished, Lady Valia. No one will raise a hand against you or your guests." Her voice had softened, smooth as polished wood. "You are quite safe."

"Yes. Yes, I know. I know that." Valia let out a long breath, and her fingers slowly unwound from the mane. "Thank you, Maedelyn."

Maedelyn inclined her head and rode back to the front of the column.

Valia turned to Dusty, her blue eyes bright and fierce. "If they give you any — if either of them says anything to you, anything at all, or looks at you in that — you tell me. Immediately. And I will take care of it."

Her jaw was set, the color flooding back into her cheeks. Dusty had no idea what she could do to two armored guardsmen, and her jaw did not care. "Thank you, ma'am."

"Valia. Please. Just Valia." She pushed a damp strand from her forehead and it fell right back. "And you asked a question. A good question. They're connected, actually, the Way Station and — it's all one answer." She straightened in her saddle, her gaze drifting somewhere past the hills. "There's nothing near Mythrandis. No nations, no kingdoms, no cities that answer to a crown. Not for a hundred miles in any direction, all the way around the lake. The academy doesn't allow it. And the Church runs the Way Stations, and the Church is a power, so — no Way Station. We have to ride the last of it. Everyone does. Princes. Emperors' daughters. Everyone gets dusty."

"The academy doesn't allow it," Dusty repeated slowly. "Who stops a king who wants to build there?"

"Oh, someone tried, a long time ago. Before I was — I don't know exactly what happened. Nobody talks about it. But nobody's tried again." She tossed it off as lightly as the weather. "And that's the point. Every noble house, every king and queen, every merchant prince, anyone with any power at all sends their children to Mythrandis. And inside those hundred miles, it doesn't matter whose children they are. It's neutral. Nobody can reach in."

The Greys rose in Dusty's mind. Men who would open a throat over a crust. Boys who cracked skulls for a copper. Two watchmen and a wall. "I don't believe that," he said. "Not that they'd send them. That they'd put it all down. Just because they're on an island."

"But they do." Valia twisted in her saddle to face him. "They have to. They're children, at first. They share halls. They take the same classes, eat in the same dining halls, get yelled at by the same professors. And they make friends. Allies. Rivals, too, but — everyone who rules anything right now came through Mythrandis. Everyone. They wrote to each other after, and they still do, twenty, forty, eighty years later. A queen writes to the man who sat beside her in alchemy. And she thinks twice before she sends her army at his border. That, and the faculty, the Headmaster, nobody wants the faculty cross with them — that's what keeps it neutral. And it keeps the world from tearing itself apart as often as it would. Wars. So many wars that never happen."

Dusty's gaze drifted north, to a horizon hidden behind the hills. "Helvanthor's been at war with the north since I was small."

Valia's mouth opened and closed. Her shoulders lifted beneath the dusty green cloak and fell. "I know. I don't — I don't have an answer for that. It's not perfect. Nothing's perfect." Her voice had dropped low. "But it would be worse without the school. I'm sure of that. Much worse."

The wheels creaked. The pony snorted and shook its shaggy mane. Princes, queens, and emperors' daughters sharing a hall, writing letters to one another forty years on. And at the far end of that table, a boy from the Greys in a secondhand linen shirt.

"How will you get me in?"

Valia blinked at him. "What?"

"Everyone there is someone. A prince, a queen's daughter. I'm no one." He said it flatly, the way he would name the price of rye. "How do you get no one into a place like that?"

"You're not no one." It burst from her quick and fierce. "You're very important. To Rinnie. And to me."

Her eyes flew wide. Color flooded up her throat to the tips of her pointed ears.

"I mean — to my work. To me as a researcher, I mean, not me as — well, also me, as a person, you're important to me as a person, because you're a person, and people are — that's not — I don't mean that I — oh, gods." She pressed the back of her wrist to her burning cheek. Then she drew a long breath, held it, released it, and fixed her eyes straight ahead between her horse's ears. "There's a lottery."

Dusty said nothing. The corner of his mouth twitched.

"The Pity Lottery, some of the — the nobles call it that. It's not a very nice name. It lets in a few common-born students every year. Children who couldn't afford it otherwise." Her hands began moving again, sketching numbers in the dusty air. "There are about — four thousand students, five, it depends on the year. And right now, just over three hundred of them aren't noble at all. Some showed a talent, or a skill, and the school heard about it and sent for them. Some have a noble sponsoring them. And some were chosen by the clergy, just — picked. At random. From the villages."

Dusty weighed each one. A candle flame and a puddle of mana. No lord had ever looked at him long enough to sponsor a meal, and the only priests he knew ladled thin soup into chipped bowls and muttered prayers over the bodies when the carts rolled through the Greys. "I don't fit any of those."

"You have me!"

A heartbeat later the pink crept back into her cheeks, but her chin lifted and she pressed on. "I'll just ask the Headmaster. Antonaez. He's — he's been something like my sponsor for a long time. Years and years. Ever since—"

She stopped. Her hands froze in the air, her face emptied, her blue eyes fixed on nothing. Dusty knew that stillness. He had seen it in the Greys on faces brushing too close to something buried. He had seen it on Rinnie when his footsteps reached the door and her hands started to shake.

"Will he really agree to it?" he asked. "Rinnie's care. A room. Healers. And letting me in besides. That's a great deal to ask of him."

Valia blinked, and her hands fell back to the reins. She turned to him with a wide, bright smile. "Oh, he'll say yes. He always says yes. And especially when I tell him I mean to study your mana. That will interest him, I'm sure of it. A black glow, with silver, that nobody's recorded in — he'll say yes."

"Then I'll be your lab rat," Dusty said, "if that's what it takes for Rinnie to get her care."

Her smile faltered. "No — not a rat, don't say rat, it's not — I'd only be running tests. Resonance tests, mostly, whether your mana carries a harmonic, if it responds to a tuning fork the way copper wire does, there's a rig with iron posts and I'd string it through — it doesn't hurt. Mostly. It's very safe. You'd hardly — well, you might feel a little buzzing, in your teeth, sometimes, and your hair might stand up, but only once, and it grows back, I mean, it lies back down, it doesn't fall out, nobody's hair has ever fallen out—"

Dusty looked at her: the dust in her tangled pink hair, the sweat beading on her brow, her blue eyes stretching wider with every word until she ran dry and sat with her mouth hanging open.

He smiled.

"I don't think you'd hurt me, Valia." He shaped her name carefully, testing the weight of it. "I only wanted you to know. I'll help you however I can. With your studies. With anything. As long as Rinnie is looked after."

Valia's mouth closed. Her eyes had gone soft and glassy. She gave one firm nod. "She will be. I'll make sure of it. She'll be safe. I promise you."

Dusty's fists loosened on the reins.

They rode on as the sun slid westward and the dust settled gold on their shoulders. The cart rattled over another rut, Gwenith's hand still moving through Rinnie's black hair, his sister sleeping on in the shade. Far behind, the two guards rode in silence. Dusty let his aching body sink into the mare's rhythm, and his eyes stayed on the cart instead of the road.

***
Five days on the road had rubbed Dusty's thighs raw and burned the back of his neck a peeling red. Twice Rinnie had begged to ride up on the mare with him, tucked against his chest with the wind whipping her black hair, laughing at every hawk and creek and cow they passed. Both times she paid for it, sleeping through the evenings and into the late mornings, and both times Maedelyn called an early halt without being asked, the cart drawn beneath a spreading oak while Gwenith fussed over pillows and broth.

Now Rinnie sat in the shade of the cart with her hands tucked beneath her blanket, her black eyes enormous.

The tents began a full mile from the walls, hundreds of them pitched in crooked rows across the trampled grass, crimson and saffron and emerald silk snapping beside heavy waxed canvas. Banners marked every camp: a golden stag on green, three silver fish on blue, a red hand gripping a sword. Thousands moved between them. Liveried servants hauled water and chests. Nobles lounged beneath awnings with goblets glinting in their hands. A knot of mages in deep blue argued on camp stools, colored light flickering around their fingers as they gestured, while guards in the Aethres colors walked the rows through the mingled smells of roasting meat, dung, and perfume.

The road carried them straight through to the city walls, forty feet of fitted gray stone crowned with square towers. The gate yawned open beneath the iron teeth of a raised portcullis, wide enough for three wagons abreast.

Aethres swallowed them. Pale stone and dark timber climbed three stories on either side, laundry and pennants strung between the upper windows. Bodies packed the clean paving shoulder to shoulder, and the cart slowed to a crawl, Dusty's mare shuffling nose to tail behind it. A lord in sable-trimmed velvet bellowed from his carriage window at a farmer whose cabbage cart had wedged across the way. Three young women in silk gowns stepped delicately around a cooper rolling barrels. A blacksmith's hammer rang somewhere close. At every corner stood guards in Mythrandis blue, white towers on their shields, the same polished plate and heavy spears as Holt and Pell. Far off above the rooftops, pale towers rose impossibly tall from the water.

Dusty's eyes darted from hands to pockets to alley mouths, and there were too many of each to follow.

Valia nudged her horse close to his, raising her voice over the din. "It's always like this," Valia called over the din, nudging her horse closer to his. "Every year, the week before the — before they open the bridge. The returning students are coming back from their summer, from the sabbatical, and all their families come with them, and the new students are waiting, and their families come with them too, and then everyone's servants and guards and—" She swept a hand at the roaring street. "This."

"Everyone camps outside the walls?"

"The ones who couldn't get a room. Which is most of them." She ducked beneath a string of pennants. "The returning students cross first. They get a day to settle back in. And then the new students, the first years, like you — you cross the bridge the day after." She straightened in her saddle, and her voice took on a measured cadence, each word set carefully in its place. "The crossing of the bridge is the first and most sacred tradition of Mythrandis. No matter your birth, no matter the size of your entourage, you walk across alone. You leave behind your titles, your wealth, and your attachments, and you arrive on the island as an equal among equals."

A laugh burst out of Dusty, short and rough. Valia's head snapped around, her brows drawing together, her full lips pushing into a pout.

"I'm sorry," he said. "I'm not laughing at you." He glanced at the lord still shouting from his carriage and back to her. "You said it like you read it from a book."

"I did read it from a book. It's in the charter." She sniffed. "It's a good line."

"It is a good line." Two footmen shoved a baker's boy out of the path of a gilded litter, and his tray of rolls spilled into the gutter. The litter swayed on. "But it doesn't matter who says it, or what book it's in. A king's son walks across that bridge, and I walk across that bridge, and on the other side he's still a king's son. And I'm still from the Greys."

Valia's hands lifted off the reins, her mouth opening, indignation bright in her blue eyes. Her hands settled back down.

"I want to argue with you," she said. "I want to tell you that you're important, and that it doesn't matter, and that everyone is — but." Her shoulders sank beneath the dusty green cloak. "But you're right. Mostly. In practice. The professors will know his name before he's even off the bridge. People will want to sit next to him at tea. And nobody will — they won't look at you like that."

She rode quiet for a few steps. Then her chin came up.

"Then I'll just have to try harder," she said. "I'll pay you the right kind of attention. So much attention that you'll feel like a king's son, even if nobody else — I'll make sure you — that you feel —"

Her voice slowed and stopped. A flush crept up from her collar.

Dusty looked back at the cart. Rinnie sat bundled beneath the canvas with Gwenith's arm around her, pointing at a monkey perched on a street performer's shoulder, a faint rose warming her pale cheeks.

He drew his sleeve across his eyes. "You already do," he said.

Valia's lips parted without a sound. Crimson swept over her cheeks to the tips of her pointed ears, and she stared straight ahead between her horse's ears, her knuckles white on the reins.

"Lady Valia." Maedelyn's black mare slid in front of them, the crowd shuffling back from her black skirts. "The inn is this way."

She turned down a narrower street without waiting, and Valia followed, silent and scarlet.

The inn stood on a corner, four stories of whitewashed stone with green shutters, a silver swan gliding across its painted sign. Carriages jammed the yard, grooms dodging between them with shouts. Dusty took in the carriages, the crowd spilling from the doors, and the hundreds of tents at their backs, and his hand tightened around the saddle horn as he swung stiffly down. "They won't have any rooms," he said. "Not with the city like this."

Maedelyn handed her reins to a waiting ostler, a boy who bowed so low his forelock nearly brushed the cobbles. She turned to Dusty, the faintest curve touching the corner of her mouth. "The Maid Corps always has a room, young man."

Dusty studied her unhurried blue eyes, her lace gloves, the ostler still bent double at her side. "Because they fear you?" he asked. "Or respect you? Or because they're grateful?"

Maedelyn turned and walked into the inn.

Pipe smoke and the sour tang of spilled ale filled the common room, every bench crammed with men laughing and slamming tankards on scarred tables. Behind the long bar a matron broad as a barrel filled four mugs at once, gray hair piled high and sleeves rolled past her thick elbows. Her eyes found Maedelyn across the room. She gave one short nod, and Maedelyn returned it.

Maedelyn fell back to Dusty's shoulder and leaned in until her breath stirred against his ear beneath the roar. "Many fear us," she murmured. "Some are grateful. But most respect us."

She began to straighten away.

"I'm one of the grateful ones," Dusty said.

Maedelyn paused, her blue eyes resting on his face. The corner of her mouth tugged once, and she pressed it flat. She led the way up a narrow staircase to the top floor.

The room ran long beneath a slanted ceiling, two windows looking out over the rooftops, four beds heaped with quilts. Dusty carried Rinnie in from the hall and laid her on the bed nearest the window, where the afternoon sun fell warm across the pillow. She sighed and burrowed into it.

Holt and Pell hovered in the hall with their helmets tucked beneath their arms.

"Thank you both," Valia said to them, the last of the pink fading from her cheeks. "For the escort. For — all of it. You can go. You're dismissed. Is that how — you're dismissed."

The guards' eyes slid past her to Maedelyn at the window and Gwenith kneeling by Rinnie's bed. Both bowed low enough that Pell's thin hair spilled across his forehead.

"Th-thank you, my lady." Holt's voice cracked. "Maids. It was an honor. A great honor."

"An honor," Pell echoed faintly.

They backed into the hall, and their boots clattered down the stairs far faster than they had climbed them.

Valia shouldered her pack while Maedelyn smoothed her apron and moved toward the door. Gwenith shot to her feet and caught her companion's sleeve. "Maedelyn." Her hazel eyes flicked to Dusty, back to Maedelyn, and to Dusty again. "You have to talk to them. About the permission. You promised." Her grip tightened. "You promised."

Maedelyn closed her eyes and let a long breath out through her nose. "I will speak to them, Gwenith."

"Today?"

"Today."

Dusty looked from one maid to the other. Gwenith caught him at it and flashed him a wide, innocent smile.

"I'll go to the Headmaster," Valia said from the doorway, her pink braid fraying loose over one shoulder. "Tonight, if he'll see me, and he will, he always — and I'll sort out Rinnie's room, and the healers, and your enrollment, and all of it. Until then, you should stay here. Both of you. Gwenith will stay with you. Don't wander off, the city's too — just stay."

"I will." Dusty stood beside the bed. "Thank you, Valia. For all of this. I mean it."

Her blue eyes softened. She nodded once and was gone, Maedelyn a silent black shape behind her, and the door clicked shut.

Gwenith settled into a chair by the far window, pulled a bit of mending from her apron pocket, and began to hum.

Dusty sat on the edge of Rinnie's bed and folded her small, cold hand inside his own. Her eyelids fluttered, a smile curving her pale lips, and her thin fingers curled around his thumb.

The city murmured faintly through the glass, a cart rattling past below, a burst of laughter, a bell ringing out the hour. Sunlight lay warm across her pillow and her face. He held her hand and watched her sleep.


***
The day dragged. Dusty ate when Gwenith pushed a plate into his hands, and he slept when Rinnie ordered him to, stretched across the bed beside hers with his new boots still laced. The rest of the hours he spent at the window, watching carriages grind through the yard below. By the next afternoon he knew the ostler's two-note whistle, the matron's laugh booming up through the floorboards, and the groan of the third stair from the top.

The stair groaned, and no footsteps followed.

The door swung open and Maedelyn stepped through, her black skirts falling still around her, lace-gloved hands folded over the white of her apron. Not a strand of her dark blue hair had slipped loose. Behind her in the hall, Valia dug elbow-deep in one of her pockets, muttering at something that refused to be found.

Gwenith's mending dropped into her lap.

Maedelyn looked at her. "You have permission."

Gwenith's shriek rattled the window glass. She crossed the room before the mending hit the floor, her stiletto heels silent on the old boards, and threw both arms around Rinnie where she sat propped against her pillows. Rinnie disappeared into black cotton and white lace with a startled squeak.

"Oh, my little sparrow. My sweet, sweet little sparrow, did you hear her? You're mine now. Well, ours, but mostly mine." Gwenith rocked her side to side, her dimples carved deep into her cheeks. "I'm going to brush your hair every single morning, and I'll count all your bites, every one, and I'll make the kitchens bake the soft bread for you and not the crusty kind that scrapes, and we'll find you the warmest blanket in the whole academy, won't we, sweet pea? Won't we?"

Rinnie's muffled voice rose from somewhere near Gwenith's collarbone. "Okay."

Dusty was on his feet, the chair legs scraping back across the floorboards. His hand had lifted halfway toward his sister before he caught it and let it fall. "Permission for what?"

Gwenith turned without letting go, Rinnie's cheek squished against her shoulder, the frayed blue ribbon knocked crooked over one ear. "Oh! You don't know. Of course you don't know, nobody told you." She beamed at him. "Maedelyn and I look after Lady Valia. We take turns in her lab. Her tea, her lunch, her books that go on the floor, making sure she goes to bed before sunrise—"

"Attempting to," Maedelyn said.

"—attempting to, and Rinnie's coming under Lady Valia's name. She's Lady Valia's guest now. Which means she's ours to look after too, if we're allowed." Gwenith squeezed. "So I asked Maedelyn to ask. And they said yes. And now I get to take care of her. Every day." She nuzzled into the crown of Rinnie's black hair. "Every single day, little sparrow."

Rinnie wriggled free enough to breathe, a warm rose flushing her pale cheeks, her black eyes gone wide. "A real maid? My own?"

"The realest one."

"Dusty." Rinnie turned those eyes on him, her cold fingers still knotted in the starched white of Gwenith's apron. "Dusty, did you hear?"

He had heard. Dusty swallowed, and it caught halfway down. Eight years of hauling crates for the price of bread, of holding his glow against her back through the winter nights, of standing in doorways with his fists ready. Now a woman in lace gloves was going to brush Rinnie's hair and count her bites, and his sister was smiling up at him, asking him to hear it.

He bent low at the waist, the way he'd watched Holt and Pell bow to the maids. "Thank you, ma'am. Thank you."

"Oh, don't ma'am me." Gwenith flapped a hand at him, her hazel eyes brimming. "I'll cry. I'll cry and Maedelyn will look at me."

Maedelyn looked at her.

"See?"

"Is it my turn?" Valia had slipped in beside Maedelyn's elbow, a fistful of papers crushed against her chest, the white tower seal pressed in blue wax across the top sheet. Her pink braid had come half-undone again, and road dust grayed the thinned elbows of her green cloak. "It's done. You're in. The Headmaster approved it, your place in the lottery, a late addition, the very last one, and the clerk's already writing your name into the ledger, and they're putting you into classes right now, today, probably right this minute—"

She thrust the papers at him. Dusty took them, the sheets stiff and cool against his scarred knuckles. The ink ran in a neat, slanted hand he couldn't read, but he knew the seal.

"He was annoyed with me." Valia's full lips pinched together. "He sat very still, the way he does. And then he sighed. At me. Twice. As if I'd asked him to drain the lake, and I only asked him for one student. One. There are five thousand of them on that island, what is one more? You'd hardly even notice." She sniffed and tugged her cloak straight. "Which was very annoying of him. Being annoyed. But it doesn't matter. He said yes. He always says yes."

Dusty turned the papers over. The back was blank. He turned them over again.

A student. He thought of the girls in silk gowns stepping delicately around the cooper's barrels, and of the lord's son behind the carriage curtain, bellowing at a farmer. None of them had knuckles split white from alley fights, or a rock bruise fading yellow and green across one cheekbone. Not ten days ago he had stood beneath Old Tobin's awning in the lower market with the rain dripping off his hood, guarding bread for bread.

"Classes," he said.

"Classes!" Valia clasped her hands beneath her chin. "Theory, and practicals, and you'll have a tutor for letters if you need one, you do need one, don't you, it's all right, lots of them do, and the first years walk the bridge in four days, so you'll have time to—" She stopped. Her hands dropped to her sides, and the color drained from her face. "Oh, gods. Your uniform."

"My uniform?"

"You need a uniform. The blazer and the tie and the slacks and boots, proper boots, and they have to be tailored, you're so—" Her hands measured the air a foot above her own head and kept climbing. "And grooming things. A comb. Soap. Tooth powder. A razor, do you shave? You must shave. And a trunk to put it all in, and I didn't — I forgot all of it, I went to the Headmaster and I sorted out the healers and I sorted out Rinnie's room and I didn't think once about—"

"Lady Valia." Maedelyn's gloved hand came to rest on her shoulder. "I will see to it."

"But the tailors will all be—"

"I will see to it."

Valia's mouth closed. Her shoulders sank a finger's width beneath the lace.

"You, however," Maedelyn continued, her hand never leaving Valia's shoulder, "must return to the academy. Tonight. The faculty meet before the crossing, and you have missed the last two." Her kohl-lined eyes held on Valia's face. "And once he crosses that bridge, he is a student. One of five thousand. You cannot play favorites."

"I won't." Valia's chin came up. "I would never. I'm very fair. I'm extremely fair."

One of Maedelyn's dark brows rose a hair's breadth and settled again.

Valia turned from it and hurried to the bed. She bent and wrapped her arms around Rinnie, Gwenith leaning back just enough to make room, and pressed her cheek into the black silk of Rinnie's hair.

"You're in good hands," she whispered. "The best hands. I promise."

Rinnie hugged her back, her thin arms tight around Valia's neck. "Thank you, Valia."

Valia straightened and turned to Dusty. She took one step toward him with her arms lifting and stopped, her hands hovering at half-height between them. She took another step, slower.

Dusty's weight rocked back onto his heels, and the papers crackled in his fist. Since the fire, only Rinnie had ever reached for him like that.

"Dusty," Rinnie said from the bed. "Oh, for— Dusty."

He looked at her. She glared back from her pillows, cheeks pink, the blue ribbon hanging crooked over her ear.

He huffed a breath through his nose and bent down.

Valia's arms wrapped around his ribs, her forehead landing against his breastbone. She smelled of rain and old paper, and of the dust of the road. His arm rose and settled across her back, stiff and careful, his broad palm resting light between her shoulder blades.

"Thank you, Valia," he said into her hair. "For all of it."

"You're very tall," she said into his shirt. "You're very, very tall, I can't even reach your — I didn't mean — I just meant you're—"

She let go and stumbled back, scarlet flooding from her collar to the tips of her pointed ears. She tugged her cloak straight, and it slid crooked. She tugged it again, then tucked a loose hank of pink hair behind her ear, and it swung right back across her eyes. She brushed at the gray dust on her elbows and smeared it across her sleeve.

"Right," she said. "Yes. Good. Goodbye. I'll — goodbye, Rinnie. Goodbye, Gwenith. I'll see you on the island. Not specially. Just normally. The normal amount."

Maedelyn inclined her head to Rinnie, then to Dusty, her deep blue eyes resting on him a moment longer. "Young man."

"Ma'am."

The corner of her mouth tugged, and she pressed it flat before following Valia out.

Dusty went to the window. Below, the two of them crossed the crowded yard, Valia's pink braid bobbing against her back, her hands already sketching shapes in the air as she talked. Maedelyn walked beside her, black skirts and white apron, and grooms and carriages shuffled aside for her without a word. They turned the corner and were gone.

"Dusty."

He turned. Rinnie sat up against her pillows, her small hands folded atop the quilt, her brows drawn down and her milk-pale lips pressed into a flat line. Their mother had worn that same face whenever he tracked mud across the floor. It sat strange on a girl who still had to take her soup in sips.

"You have to study," she said. "Properly. You can't spend all your time worrying about me, because I have Gwenith now, and she's going to brush my hair."

"Every single morning," Gwenith said, stooping to gather her mending from the floor.

"So you have to focus on your classes and not me." Rinnie's lower lip wobbled once before she firmed it. "But you can't forget to visit. On rest days. Every rest day."

Dusty crossed the room and sat on the edge of her bed, the old ropes creaking beneath his weight. He reached out and straightened the blue ribbon over her ear. "I'm not leaving yet. I've still got four days."

"I know."

"And I'll visit."

"Every rest day."

"Every rest day."

She nodded once and burrowed back down into the quilts, the afternoon sun lying warm across her pillow.

Dusty looked past her, out the window. Beyond the rooftops of Aethres and the gray smoke rising from a thousand chimneys, the lake lay flat and silver beneath the afternoon sun. Out on the water rose the island, the academy sprawled across it in pale towers climbing one above another, their high windows catching the sunlight and throwing it back across the lake like scattered coins.

***
The morning sun hung a hand's width above the eastern rooftops of Aethres, burning the last ribbons of mist from the lake. Dusty stood at the foot of the bridge, a small trunk at his heels and a starched collar pinching at his throat.

Maedelyn had seen to everything, as she'd said she would. A barber with cold scissors and warm hands had come to the Silver Swan the night before, cutting the black ragged mess of Dusty's hair above his ears and sweeping the rest back from his brow with a comb and a thumb of oil that smelled of cedar. The scent clung to him still, and every few breaths he caught it and turned his head, searching for its source.

The uniform had arrived at dawn wrapped in brown paper and string. Black slacks pressed to a crease sharp enough to slice bread. A black undershirt of cotton softer than any blanket he'd slept beneath. A tie of deep blue silk that Gwenith had knotted at his throat after watching him fumble it twice. Over it all hung a blazer of dark navy wool, the white tower of Mythrandis stitched in silver thread above his heart, and below it, black boots polished to a mirror shine, the leather still stiff at the ankles.

It all fit. The sleeves reached his wrists, and the shoulders sat where his shoulders sat, and the slacks didn't ride an inch above his ankles the way every pair of secondhand trousers he'd ever owned had done. Still his fingers kept tugging at the cuffs, and his neck kept rolling against the collar, his body waiting for a seam to split.

Aethres had emptied itself onto the shore.

Hundreds of families crowded the broad stone plaza before the bridge, their silks glittering in the morning light. Mothers swept along in gowns of sapphire and saffron, pearls weighting their hems. Fathers stood stiff in brocade doublets threaded with gold, rings fat on every finger, jeweled sword hilts at their hips that had never known a callus. Servants in house colors hovered at the edges with parasols and hats and small ribboned dogs tucked beneath their arms. Perfume hung thick over the plaza, cut with horse sweat and the sweet smoke of a chestnut vendor who had pushed his cart too close to the gentry.

At the heart of every knot of silk stood a student in a colored blazer.

A girl in crimson knelt in her pleated black skirt, a white-haired grandmother bending over her gilded cane to kiss both her cheeks. A boy in dark green stood rigid as his father gripped his shoulders and spoke low into his face, and when the father's hands fell away the boy's chin trembled. Nearby a mother in lavender silk sobbed into a lace handkerchief, her broad-shouldered son patting her back with a face burned red to the ears, his eyes fixed past her on the bridge.

One by one the students broke from their families and walked out onto the stone, trunks rumbling behind them on iron-shod wheels, some hauled by leather straps, others stacked three high on handcarts and dragged by sweating boys who had likely never dragged anything in their lives. As each family stepped back from the edge, another pressed forward into the gap. The plaza couldn't hold them all, and so they came in waves, silk and gold surging to the foot of the bridge and ebbing back like the lake lapping at the pilings below.

Dusty watched the hands. Rings, purses, jeweled hilts, fat coin pouches tied to belts with a single careless knot. None of these people had ever been robbed, or gone a night hungry.

Then he spotted one who had.

A girl in a plain blue blazer stood alone by the parapet, her pleated skirt a finger too long for the fashion, her socks white cotton instead of silk. A summer of sun had browned her face and hands, her forearms thick and corded from work, her hair chopped blunt at the jaw without a ribbon in it. Both her hands gripped the handle of a battered wooden trunk, and nobody bent to kiss her cheeks.

Farther along stood a boy with a farmer's shoulders and a peeling red nose, his green blazer straining at the seams, and beyond him a thin girl with ink-stained fingers, clutching a canvas bag in place of a trunk, her eyes darting from face to face the way Dusty's did. Four or five of them, scattered through the silk like burrs caught in a fine cloak, and they kept their eyes on the bridge and off one another.

Dusty turned and looked back over the rooftops of Aethres.

He found it on the rise above the market: a narrow house of honey-colored brick, three stories tall, its window boxes spilling red geraniums over the sills. Gwenith had found it and furnished it in two days, with an academy healer due at its door each morning. He counted up to the third floor, to the window on the left beneath the eave, where the morning sun would fall first across the bed.

The white curtains hung still behind the glass, and the window stood empty.

She'd be asleep. She had spent yesterday touching everything in her new room, the washstand, the wardrobe, the painted birds circling the ceiling, pestering Gwenith for the name of each one. She would pay for it today. She'd sleep past noon, and Gwenith would count her bites at lunch, and the bread would be soft.

She was in good hands.

Dusty took in the crowd one last time, the silk and the gold and the weeping mothers, and bent for the handle of his trunk.

Across the plaza, a black dress stood still in the moving crowd.

Gwenith waited beside a stone bollard at the plaza's edge, her lace-gloved hands folded over her white apron, her light brown ponytail stirring in the breeze off the lake. The crowd parted around her without brushing her sleeve. Her hazel eyes found his and locked.

A ribbon trailed from her gloved fingers, blue and frayed at both ends, the dye worn pale along the fold where it had been tied and untied a thousand times. Rinnie's ribbon.

Gwenith lifted her chin in a small, firm nod, then turned and slipped back into the crowd, vanishing between one family and the next.

The leather of the trunk handle creaked in Dusty's fist. Heat pricked behind his eyes, and he blinked it back hard, once and again, swallowing until the knot in his throat loosened enough to breathe past, a thousand nobles in silk all around him.

He drew in a breath of lake water and cedar oil and stepped onto the bridge.

The stone lay smooth beneath his new boots, pale gray and worn into shallow grooves by centuries of wheels and feet. The bridge ran wide enough for four carriages abreast, its waist-high parapets carved with twisting vines, a Guard standing at attention every fifty paces with his spear grounded and a blue shield on his arm. Dusty fell into the stream of blazers, crimson and green and violet and gold, and let it carry him out over the water.

The bridge stretched on longer than any street in Helvanthor, farther than the walk from the Greys to the lower market and back. The lake lay flat and silver on either side, the wind rising off it cool against his freshly shaven jaw. Behind him a girl laughed too loudly. Ahead, a boy complained to no one about a bad wheel on his trunk.

The academy grew with every step. From the window of the Silver Swan it had been a scatter of pale towers at the edge of sight, light glinting across the water. Now it climbed, white stone and pale gold, slender spires stacked one above another, and at its center the tallest of them speared so high into the blue that Dusty had to tip his head back to find its peak. Gardens spilled green down terraced hillsides, rows of trees lined white walls, and sunlight flashed off a thousand panes of glass. By the time his boots reached the far end of the bridge, the towers had swallowed the sky.

The bridge opened onto a wide stone court at the island's edge, and the court was full of Maids.

Dozens of them moved through the flood of arriving students in black dresses and white aprons, lace gloves and stiletto heels that made no sound against the flagstones. They worked the crowd like herders working sheep through a gate, a touch to an elbow, a gesture, a quiet word, and the students sorted themselves beneath their hands without a single voice raised. A Maid with silver hair pinned in a tight bun counted students off with two fingers, twenty-eight, twenty-nine, thirty, and turned the group away through an arch, and another Maid stepped into her place and began counting anew.

A tall Maid with a sweep of copper-red hair stopped in front of Dusty, heavy kohl framing her gray eyes. She had to tip her chin up a long way to meet his gaze. Those eyes traveled once from his polished boots to his combed hair and back again, her painted lips unmoving.

"This way, please."

Two gloved fingers touched his elbow and steered him, his trunk rumbling behind, into a cluster of blazers gathering by the wall. A boy in crimson glanced at him, then at his plain trunk, and turned his shoulder. Two girls in violet whispered behind their hands. The copper-haired Maid counted to thirty and led them away.

They passed beneath one arched gate and then another, through the shadow of a building whose cool walls breathed old stone and floor wax, then down a long colonnade where sunlight fell in bright bars between white pillars. Dusty counted his steps and marked each turn, left at the fountain with the bronze fish, right beneath the arch with the carved owl, always knowing the way back out.

The colonnade opened into a courtyard of white flagstones ringed by lime trees, a fountain murmuring at its heart. Their Maid stopped before it and turned, hands folding over her apron, and the thirty of them shuffled into a loose half circle, their whispers falling away.

"Welcome to Mythrandis." Her voice carried low and even to every corner of the courtyard, without any strain Dusty could see. "I am Maid Oriel. For the remainder of today, you are in my care. Tomorrow you are in your own."

A boy near the front snickered. Oriel's gray eyes slid to him and rested there until his mouth snapped shut and his ears went pink.

"Your days will be kept as follows. Breakfast is served at seven o'clock in the dining halls. Your first class begins at eight. Classes and study sessions continue through the morning. Luncheon is at noon. Classes end at three o'clock. At four, tea is served. At six, dinner. Curfew is ten o'clock, and it is not a suggestion." The fountain splashed into the silence she left. "Your class schedules and room assignments will be delivered to your dormitories by the Maid Corps. You will not ask who decided them. You will not ask to trade them."

A girl in gold raised her hand, bracelets clinking at her wrist. "And if my room is unsuitable?"

"It will not be," Oriel said.

The girl's hand sank back to her side.

"On this island, all students are equal. You have heard this. You walked across the bridge to prove it." Oriel's gaze swept the half circle, from silk-cuffed wrists to sun-browned knuckles, her painted face never shifting. "All actions must be with Beauty, Elegance, and Dignity above all else. Remember it. Come."

The rest of the day passed beneath their feet. Oriel led them through halls where the ceilings rose so high their footsteps echoed back as whispers, and across lawns so green and even that Dusty kept waiting for someone to shout at him for walking on them. Libraries climbed three stories, their ladders sliding on brass rails. Lecture halls stepped down in rings of benches toward a single lectern. Long oak tables in the dining halls gleamed with a honeyed polish, and in a practice yard dust hung gold in the sunlight to the clack of wooden swords. Roses and lilies and flowers he had no names for spilled from every bed, the air heavy and sweet with them, and white marble statues stood watch in the alcoves without a crack or a chip or a smear of bird lime among them. Dusty kept searching for the rot beneath it all, the gutter, the alley where the bodies went, and found only polished stone.

Other groups crossed their path throughout the day, thirty blazers trailing behind each black dress. Once, in a long gallery lined with gilt-framed portraits, two groups passed close enough to brush sleeves and both stumbled to a halt. A boy in Dusty's group had stopped dead, and across from him stood another boy in the same dark green, with the same sharp nose and heavy brow. Brothers, by the look of them. Neither spoke. Both Maids turned their heads at the same moment, and both groups moved on.

Older students watched from windows, balconies and benches, the returning years who had crossed the day before, leaning on railings and pointing and calling out to the first years they knew. A girl in a violet blazer with a black silk sash cinched at her waist watched them pass from the top of a stair, arms folded, brow furrowed in thought. A young man in a white shawl strode past with a stack of papers beneath his arm and a knot of students trailing after him, and every head in Dusty's group turned to follow.

Dusty kept his hands where people could see them and his eyes moving. Few eyes lingered on him.

The sun had slid well past its peak by the time Oriel had shed her students one by one at the northern dormitories, pale stone halls roofed in slate, ivy climbing their walls. She stopped at the third of them and turned to Dusty.

"Up the stairs. Fourth floor. The seventh door on your left."

"Thank you, ma'am."

Her gray eyes rested on him a breath longer than they had on the others before she inclined her head. She turned and walked away with the last three blazers behind her, her heels silent on the stone.

Dusty hauled his trunk up four flights of oak stairs worn hollow at their centers, down a long hall carpeted in blue, past six doors to the seventh. A small brass plate beside the frame bore a single engraved word: DUSTY. No family name followed it. He had none to give them.

The key sat waiting in the lock. He turned it and stepped inside.

Afternoon light poured through a tall south-facing window, thick and gold across a floor of honey-colored boards polished to a soft sheen, the walls pale plaster. A bed stood against the wall beneath a heaped blue wool blanket, its mattress fat with feathers. Beneath the window stood a desk of dark oak with a lamp, a stack of blank paper and a bottle of ink laid out upon it. An armoire in the corner stood with its doors open on a row of empty brass hooks. Through a second door, white tile gleamed around a basin and pitcher and a copper tub.

Dusty stood beside his trunk in the doorway and didn't move.

He'd been six, maybe seven, the last time a door had locked behind him. Two rooms above a cooper's shop in the lower city, their one window split corner to corner by a crack, the stove that smoked, the roof that dripped onto the corner where his mother kept the flour. Rinnie asleep in a rag-lined crate beside the stove, and his father mending boots at the table by candlelight, singing off-key. The place had been damp and cramped and stinking of pitch from the cooperage below, and Dusty had thought it the finest place in the world. The rent had gone up, and the Greys had swallowed them, and then the coughing came to their street with the guards close behind it.

This room was bigger than both of those rooms put together. His fingers found the doorframe and closed around the smooth painted wood until his knuckles went white.

A bell tolled across the island, deep and clear, rolling out over the slate roofs and the gardens and the silver lake. Once. Twice. Three times. Four. Tea.

Dusty let go of the frame. He dragged his trunk to the foot of the bed, straightened his blazer, tugged at his cuffs, and smoothed a hand over his cedar-scented hair, then stepped out into the hall and locked the door behind him.

Along the corridor, doors were swinging open, laughter spilling out with voices calling names, friends from home falling into step with one another. A boy in gold slung an arm over another boy's shoulders and they clattered down the stairs together, arguing over something that left them both grinning.

Dusty followed them down, alone, his new boots loud against the old oak steps. He knew what waited below: a crowded hall of a hundred tables, and a seat at the end of one of them where nobody would sit beside him.

He'd eaten alone before.

***
The lecture hall sank in tiered rings of dark oak benches toward a stage at the bottom, where morning light slanted through tall windows and pooled across a slate board as wide as a hay wagon. Dusty took the last seat on the highest ring with his back against the wall, the leather notebook Maedelyn had packed in his trunk open across his knee, a pencil stub pinched between his scarred fingers. Below him fifty blazers filled the benches in a rustle of wool and whispers, the nobles settling into the front rings as though the oak had been carved with their names.

The door at the bottom of the hall slammed open, cracking against the wall.

The man who ducked through it had to turn his shoulders to fit the frame. He stood taller than Dusty and half again as broad, his russet beard cut square across a barrel chest, sleeves shoved past his elbows over forearms as thick as Dusty's thighs. The chalk in his fist looked no bigger than a grain of rice.

"GOOD MORNING!"

The windows rattled in their frames, and a girl in the second row dropped her pen clattering to the floor.

"I am Professor Brannoch, and this is Introduction to Magic, and if any of you are in the wrong room, now is the time to slink out with your dignity." He waited, teeth flashing white through the russet beard, while nobody moved. "Splendid! Then let us begin with the only thing that matters."

He turned to the slate and scrawled a single word in letters a foot tall, the chalk shrieking and snapping in half on the final stroke. MANA. He flicked the broken stub over his shoulder without looking.

"Every one of you has it. The prince and the ploughboy. The duchess and the girl who scrubbed her floors. It lives in you the way blood lives in you, and it runs out of you in one of three ways." He slammed three strokes beneath the word, each gouging a white scar across the slate. "Martial. Elemental. Healing."

He jabbed the first with a finger the size of a sausage.

"Martial mana turns inward. It makes you stronger, faster, harder to kill. Your fists hit like hammers. Your skin turns a blade. You'll see no glow from a martial fighter, not a flicker, only the results, and by the time you see the results you'll be picking yourself up off the floor." His finger slid to the second stroke. "Elemental turns outward. Fire, water, wind, stone, light. It shapes the world around you. Those of you with elemental leanings will see your hands glow when you work, and every one of you glows a different color, and no, you don't get to pick it." He rapped the third. "Healing goes into others. Into flesh, into blood, into bone. Your hands glow for this as well. It's the hardest of the three to learn and the hardest to master, and it's the one you'll all be screaming for the first time you break an arm in the practice yard."

Laughter scattered across the benches, and Brannoch's grin spread wider.

"Now." He planted both fists on the lectern and leaned over it, the old wood groaning beneath his weight. "Some of you arrived here certain you'll be a battlemage or a swordmaster or a great healer of kings. Let me spare you some tears. You don't choose your alignment. You're born leaning one way, the way you're born left-handed or right. Most of you have a disposition for one of the three, and you'll be good at that one and middling at the others. A rare few of you will lean two ways at once." He raised two thick fingers. "Blended. We'll fight over you in the faculty lounge."

Dusty's pencil hovered over the page.

He wrote the word slowly, HEALING, in the square block letters he'd pieced together from the painted shop signs of the lower market, and underlined it twice. Healing was the bargain. Valia had backed him against a wall in the Greys, her blue eyes wide and the smell of rain and old paper hanging about her, and told him his hands could hold mana steady without ever stopping, that a healer needed nothing more, that someday those same hands could help Rinnie.

But his hands made fire. Only a candle's worth, orange wrapped in black haze and edged in silver, but fire all the same. It had seared the back of a sick man's hand in the gutter beneath Old Tobin's awning, and it had burned across a boy's eyes in the black-water alleys of the Greys. Fire was elemental. Brannoch had just said so.

Beneath HEALING he wrote ASK VALIA in letters twice as large, and pressed a box around it hard enough to dent the page.

"Next!" Brannoch's fist banged the slate, sifting white dust from the chalk tray. "How much of it do you have? That, my young friends, is not up to you either." He chalked a small circle onto the board. "Some of you carry a well. Deep enough to draw from, not deep enough to swim in." Beside it, a larger circle. "Some of you carry a pond." Then a wide, sweeping oval that ran nearly to the slate's edge. "A few of you carry a lake. And once in a very great while," he flung his arms wide enough to span half the stage, "someone walks across that bridge carrying an ocean. Those ones, you do not annoy."

His arms dropped to his sides.

"Whatever you carry, you carry. You're born with it. It'll deepen some with practice and years, but a well does not become a lake because it wishes very hard. And when you pour it out?" He turned his great hand over and shook it, emptying an invisible cup onto the stage. "You're empty. Your legs go soft. Your eyes won't focus. Your head pounds like a smithy. Push past that and your body simply stops, and you'll wake up in the Solarium with a healer scolding you and no memory of how you got there. Sleep fills it back. Food fills it back. A full day of rest, give or take, and you're brimming again." He straightened, his booming voice dropping so low the back rows leaned forward on their benches to catch it. "So you manage it. Every class, every duel, every spell. The student who empties himself showing off at noon is the student who can't lift a candle at six. Learn how much you have, and learn how much you've spent. It matters more than anything else I'll teach you this year."

Dusty looked down at his hand resting on the open notebook.

He had never been empty. He had held his flame against Rinnie's back from dusk until gray light seeped through the shutters, winter after winter, her shivering easing beneath his palm, and his legs had never once gone soft. He had lit it twenty-three times in a single morning beneath Tobin's awning with the elf counting every one, then fought ten children in the Greys until his body gave out, the flame still burning steady behind his breastbone.

A well does not become a lake because it wishes very hard. His was no well at all, only a candle that had never guttered out.

His fingers curled into a fist on his knee. Around him fifty pencils scratched across fifty pages, and nobody glanced up at the tall boy on the top bench whose pencil had gone still.

***
History of the World met in a narrow hall of cold gray stone beneath a ceiling painted with faded maps. The lecturer, a stooped old man with a voice like dry leaves skittering on cobbles, read from a cracked leather ledger without once lifting his eyes. Empires rose and crumbled across the hour, kings were crowned and poisoned, and borders crept across the painted ceiling and crept back again, treaties signed at the tables of long-dead ministers whose names meant nothing to Dusty.

He wrote it all down, the pencil dragging slow in his grip, each letter pressed hard and square, every name spelled the way it sounded because he knew no other way to spell it. When the old man chalked a word onto the board, Dusty copied it stroke by stroke the way he'd once copied the baker's sign onto a scrap of slate in the Greys. By the end of the hour four pages lay filled, his fingers cramped into a claw around the pencil, and he couldn't have repeated half of what was on them.

He blew the gray graphite dust from the last page and closed the notebook with care. Valia had promised a tutor for letters. He wrote that at the bottom of his list, too.

***
The hall for Magical Application dwarfed Brannoch's three times over, a great half bowl of stone benches rising from a sand-floored pit, and every seat was taken. Nearly two hundred students packed the rings shoulder to shoulder, the air hanging warm and close with wool and scented oils and nervous sweat. Dusty wedged himself onto the end of a bench near the top, his knees jammed against the backs of the row in front.

Below, alone in the pit, stood an elf, slender and straight-backed, her silver hair cropped close to her skull, her robes a severe charcoal gray. She waited without a word until the whole hall had hushed on its own, and then she waited a little longer.

"I am Professor Venn." Her voice was soft, yet it reached the top bench as clear as if she stood beside him. "Professor Brannoch has told you what mana is. I will teach you to touch it. Close your eyes."

Two hundred pairs of eyes fell shut. Dusty kept his half-open, watching the room through his lashes.

"Breathe in through your nose. Hold. Out through the mouth. Again." The hall filled with the long, slow rush of breathing. "Now find the place just beneath your breastbone. Do not reach for it. Let your attention settle there, the way dust settles in still water. There is warmth there. For some of you it will feel like a coal. For others, like sun on stone. It has always been there. You have simply never been told to look."

Dusty had no need to look. His flame sat where it always sat, small and steady beneath his breastbone, the same warmth he'd pressed into Rinnie's back through a hundred winter nights.

"Now," Venn said, "coax it outward. Toward your hands. Slowly."

Light bloomed across the hall, scattered sparks at first and then whole clusters bursting from palms up and down the rings. A boy two rows down gasped as his fingers flared a vivid green. A girl near the pit laughed aloud, cupping a soft rose glow like a moth caught between her hands. Blue and amber and violet and white rippled through the tiers, some burning bright and steady, some sputtering out the moment they appeared, while here and there a student sat with eyes squeezed shut and empty hands, faces flushed red with strain.

Dusty kept his palms pressed flat against his knees and his flame banked beneath his breastbone.

Venn paced the rim of the pit with her hands folded behind her back, her pale eyes sweeping the tiers, and her own hands gave off no light at all.

"Enough. Let it settle." The glows faded one by one, and a murmur rolled through the hall. "Those of you who found it, good. Those of you who did not, you will. Some of you arrived ahead of the others. That is no great virtue, only an accident of tutors and family. Next class, we will test each of you and learn where your alignments lie."

A hand shot up in the second row, a gold signet ring flashing on the finger of a boy in a crimson blazer. "Professor. My family's tutors confirmed my alignment when I was eight. Elemental, with a leaning toward fire. Must I still sit the test?"

Venn's pale eyes rested on him until his hand sank back to the bench. "Some of you are ahead," she said. "Others are not. All of you will be tested, and all of you will be placed into your alignment classes afterward, according to what the test shows. Not according to what your tutors told your mother." She turned her back on him. "Dismissed."

Laughter rolled up the benches, and the boy in crimson sank lower, red flooding up to his collar.

***
The dining hall roared beneath its iron chandeliers, long oak tables running the length of the room, servers in white aprons carrying out platters faster than the students could strip them bare. Butter glistened on roast chicken. Brown loaves steamed when they tore. Cold cider sweated beads down the sides of pewter pitchers and onto the wood.

Dusty took the end of a table near the wall. The two boys seated at the far end had glanced at him when he sat, then slid further up the bench, leaving four feet of empty oak between his elbow and the nearest body.

He filled his plate and cleaned it, then filled it again.

You look like a skeleton. Eat. Valia's ears had burned scarlet when she said it, her fork trembling over her plate at the Gilded Pheasant, and Rinnie had counted his plates aloud across the table, five of them, grinning at him over the rim of her soup bowl.

He tore another hunk of bread, dragged it through the thick brown gravy, and reached for more chicken. Around his fourth plate, a pair of girls across the aisle set their forks down to watch, and when he reached for the fifth, one of them began counting under her breath.

Dusty wiped his mouth, laid his fork across the empty plate, and thought Rinnie would have been proud of him.

***
The potions hall smelled of green things and burning. Long worktables filled a room beneath a vaulted ceiling blackened by generations of smoke, copper basins and glass flasks set out beside small iron braziers gone cold. Jars of powders and pickled things in yellow brine climbed the shelves to the rafters, bundles of dried herbs hanging head-down from the beams. Students of every year crammed the room, the older ones in faded blazers with ink-stained cuffs, the first years crisp and new and clustered near the door.

Dusty stood just inside, holding the schedule a maid had slipped beneath his door that morning. POTIONS: FOUNDATIONS, it read, in a neat hand he'd spent a quarter hour sounding out. Nobody from his morning classes had followed him here, and he had no idea why his name had been put on this room. He added it to the list at the back of his notebook, below the tutor and the fire. Ask Valia.

Every stool held a body, and the few that didn't were claimed by satchels and blazers slung across them, older students leaning against the walls and shelves. Dusty's eyes searched the room for an empty seat and came up with nothing, his fingers tightening on the schedule until the paper crinkled.

"You! Hey! Tall one!"

Near the middle of the room a girl had risen half out of her seat, waving both arms over her head as though flagging down a ship from the shore.

"Yes, you! You're the tallest one here, it's definitely you! Over here, there's room, come on!"

Heads swiveled toward him. Dusty glanced behind him, then back, and she was still waving.

There was room. He saw it as he wound down between the tables: three empty stools to her left and two to her right, a bare island of oak in the middle of a packed room. At the next table the students had nudged their stools an inch away from its edge, and two girls leaning against the shelf beside it had chosen to stand. Dusty marked it the way he marked alley mouths and empty hands, and he sat down on the stool beside her.

"Thank you," he said.

"Oh, thank you! You saved me. I was about to start talking to the jars." She swung toward him on her stool, her knees bumping against his. "Hi! Hello. I'm Auriel. Auriel Landsdon."

She was gold from crown to heel. Thick hair fell loose down her back to the curve of her hips, the color of honey held up to a window, and her eyes were a warm burnished amber that caught the brazier light and held it. Her face was round and soft, full-cheeked above a small rounded chin, her generous mouth already curved into a smile. She was short and soft-built, filling out the white of her blazer until the buttons strained across her chest, her black pleated skirt flaring over the full swell of her hips. Knotted crooked at her waist hung a sash of red silk stitched with gold thread, the lopsided bow the only thing on her that clashed with the school.

"Dusty," he said.

"Dusty! I love it. Dusty what?"

"Just Dusty."

"Just Dusty." She rolled it over her tongue, tasting it. "That's wonderful. That's a name people remember. You're a first year, aren't you? You have to be, your blazer still has the fold marks in it. Did you cross yesterday? Isn't the bridge the longest walk in the whole world? My feet hurt for two days after mine, and I'm told I complained the entire time, which is a lie, I only complained for most of it. Where are you from? Did you come far?"

"Helvanthor."

"Helvanthor! That's so far. Did you take a Way Station? I love the Way Stations, they sing when they fire, did you hear it? Like a bell the size of a house." She rushed on without leaving him a gap to answer. "What's your alignment? Oh, you won't know yet, they haven't tested you. What do you think it'll be? You look martial. You're so tall. Are you martial? You don't have to tell me. You can tell me."

Dusty opened his mouth, then shut it again. Grown men in the Greys had come at him with cudgels and given him less trouble.

"I don't know yet," he said. "I'm hoping for healing."

Her whole face brightened. "Healing! That's the best one. Everyone says martial is the best one, but everyone is wrong." She leaned in close, dropping her voice only a little. "Don't tell Professor Brannoch I said that. He'll cry, and then he'll make you do push-ups."

The corner of Dusty's mouth twitched. "I won't tell him."

"Good. You're trustworthy. I can tell. I'm very good at telling." She beamed up at him and drew breath to say more.

A pot lid clanged against a table at the front of the room. Auriel snapped her mouth shut and spun forward on her stool.

The lecturer was a thin gray woman in a leather apron scorched black along its hem, her spectacles pushed up into a frizz of iron-colored hair, her fingertips stained a permanent yellow-brown.

"Professor Mottram," Auriel whispered out of the side of her mouth. "She's wonderful. She's terrifying."

The professor offered no introduction. She pulled a flask from the shelf at her back, its contents a blue so vivid it seemed to glow, and raised it to the window light.

"This is a mana restorative," she said, dry and clipped. "It is not a miracle. Drink it empty and it will fill perhaps a third of your reserve, and you will have a headache for the rest of the afternoon, and if you drink three in a day you will spend the night vomiting." She set it down and lifted a second flask, thick and rust red. "This is a wound draught. It closes cuts, it knits torn muscle. It does nothing for a fever, nothing for a cough, nothing for a poison. Every potion on these shelves does one thing, and only that thing, and does it well if you brew it right." Her eyes swept the room over the rims of her spectacles. "Anyone who wants a potion that cures everything may leave now and go become a charlatan in Aethres. You will make a great deal more money."

A few of the older students laughed, and her face never moved.

She spent the rest of the hour laying out the year ahead, fever tinctures and sleeping draughts, antidotes each matched to a single poison, salves for burns and poultices for bruising, and a lung tonic she called Coughwort before making the whole room repeat it back to her. Dusty's pencil scraped along as fast as it could manage, which was not fast, and he dug a line beneath the tonic for the lungs, and the tincture for fevers, and the draught for weakness of the blood.

Rinnie coughed in the night, sometimes, and her hands were always cold.

He dug the lines in deeper, until the graphite shone.

Beside him Auriel's pen flowed across her page in a looping, lovely hand, little flowers blooming in the margins, and once, when Mottram's back was turned, she tipped her notebook toward him to show a tiny sketch of Professor Brannoch straining through push-ups. Dusty stared hard at his own page after that, his jaw clenched and his shoulders shaking.

The pot lid clanged again, ending the hour.

Stools scraped and chatter erupted across the room. Dusty closed his notebook and stood, and Auriel rose with him, her mouth opening bright and ready and then falling shut.

For half a breath she stood frozen. Her hand drifted toward his sleeve and hung there, fingers curling in the air, then dropped back to her side, her smile never slipping.

"Do you—" She stopped. Started again, brighter. "Would you want to have tea with me? Today? At four? You don't have to. You probably have plans already, everyone has plans on the first day, it's fine if you—"

Dusty stared down at her. Nobody had ever asked him to anything, except to leave.

"Yes," he said. "I'd like that."

Her face lit, warmth flooding gold from her chin to her hairline, her amber eyes turning bright and wet.

"Really? Wonderful! Oh, that's wonderful. The blue courtyard, the one with the swan statue. Four o'clock. Don't be late, the good cakes go first!"

She scooped her books against her chest and was gone before he could draw breath to ask, darting between the worktables and out the door with her red sash streaming behind her.

Dusty stood beside the row of empty stools with his notebook in his hand. He'd walked past two dozen courtyards on yesterday's tour and couldn't recall the color of a single one. He'd find it. He'd found worse places, with less to go on.

He turned for the door and stopped.

The room had fallen quieter than it should have. A knot of third years by the shelves stood with their heads bent close, eyes fixed on him. At the next table two girls had gone silent, their gazes flicking from him to the doorway where Auriel had vanished and back again. One leaned over and whispered behind her hand, and the other shook her head slowly, lips pressed thin.

Dusty tucked the notebook beneath his arm and walked out past them, his face blank, his shoulders loose.

Maybe first years didn't take tea with second years. Maybe it was a rule written in a charter somewhere, one that everybody born to silk already knew.

He'd add it to the list.

***
Dusty left the potions hall with his satchel slung across his chest, the strap biting into his shoulder beneath the leather notebook and its crammed pages. He'd study them tonight. He'd sit at the dark oak desk beneath his window with the lamp turned high and sound out every word one slow syllable at a time, the way he'd once puzzled out the painted signs above the lower market stalls, until the names of dead kings and fever tinctures held still on the page and meant something. It would take him half the night, while everyone else read theirs in a quarter hour and went to bed.

He passed beneath a colonnade of white pillars, the afternoon sun laying long bars of gold across the flagstones, and came out into a courtyard paved in pale yellow stone, an old oak spreading its limbs over a ring of benches. He crossed it and ducked through an arch into the next, gray walls ringed with lavender, fat bees droning among the purple spikes. Neither one was blue.

A tutor. Valia had said it herself in her rush, you'll have a tutor for letters if you need one, you do need one, don't you, and he did, more than he'd ever needed the boots. But she'd already bought him the boots. She'd already dragged a tailor out of bed in Helvanthor, and found Rinnie a room with a window for the sun, and gone to the Headmaster and let him sigh at her twice. Every line Dusty scratched into the back of his notebook was one more thing he'd be setting in her arms to carry. ASK VALIA. Healing, and the fire. Potions, and why. A tutor. He'd boxed each one so hard the pencil had dented through to the next page.

He would wait. When she called him to her lab in the east wing with its copper wire strung between iron posts, he'd lay the whole list out for her at once, and after that he'd leave her be. He had no idea when that would be. Tomorrow, or next week. She'd promised to call for him, and she'd promised not to play favorites, and Maedelyn had lifted one dark brow at that.

A bell rolled out over the rooftops, deep and bronze, humming up through the stone beneath his boots, four long strokes.

Dusty pulled up short in the middle of a courtyard of green marble. He'd wandered a full hour between arches and colonnades without a scrap of blue, and Auriel had told him not to be late, the good cakes go first.

He lengthened his stride.

The academy had changed while he walked. In the green marble courtyard a white canopy had sprung up over a long table where none had stood when he'd crossed it the first time, a silver tea service steaming at its center beside tiered plates of iced cakes and sugar-dusted shortbread, a dozen students gathered around it. Past the next arch three girls in violet lounged on a blanket spread across the grass with porcelain cups balanced on their knees, their laughter light and tinkling. Beneath a pergola wound thick with wisteria, a boy in gold held court at the head of a table of eight, hands sweeping through the air, the others leaning in to catch every word while their tea went cold in front of them.

A Maid stood at the edge of every courtyard.

They waited in the corners and beneath the arches in black dresses and white aprons, lace-gloved hands folded, faces still. One refilled a teapot from a silver kettle and never once took her eyes off the table while she poured. Another stood so motionless beside a column that Dusty was three strides past before he saw her at all, and her gaze prickled along the back of his neck for ten strides more.

They'd carried all of it out, the tables and canopies and tiered plates and the kettles still steaming, into a hundred courtyards across the island inside the space of a single bell. Somebody had known the boy in gold would want eight chairs beneath the wisteria and not six, and that the girls in violet would take the grass instead of the benches. Dusty tried to picture the kitchen it would take, and the ledger that tracked it all, and the Maid who read the ledger, and his mind came up as empty as a picked pocket.

The whole island had stopped for tea. No bells rang for classes, no carts rattled over the flagstones, and nobody hurried anywhere but him. Five thousand students sat down at once to sip from painted porcelain and nibble at cakes and decide who sat beside whom, whose invitation counted and whose counted for nothing. Rinnie had come away from the women's room at the Gilded Pheasant giggling that Gwenith brewed very important tea in Valia's lab, and Dusty had taken it for a joke.

He passed a courtyard of red brick, and another of white, and one with a rearing bronze horse, and one where a stone woman held up a set of scales. In the next a pond full of fat orange carp gulped at the crumbs a girl in green was flicking from her fingertips. No blue walls, and no swan.

His jaw ached. He'd been grinding his teeth.

This was stupid, a whole hour of daylight wasted when he could have been at his desk with the lamp lit, sounding out Coughwort and fever tincture and weakness of the blood. Instead he was walking circles to drink tea and eat cake with a girl he'd met three hours ago, because somebody somewhere had decided this was how every day on the island had to end.

Laughter burst from the courtyard to his right, high and bright and hollow as a tin cup. A boy in crimson had said something, and the four girls around him laughed on cue, hands fluttering over their painted mouths, eyes cutting sideways to see who else laughed and for how long. His signet ring flashed gold as he raised his cup. It might have been the same ring from the second row of Venn's class. They all wore rings.

Dusty's hands had balled into fists at his sides. He made them open.

The tea had nothing to do with it. It was the four pages of history he couldn't read without his lips moving, and the empty stools on either side of Auriel, and the girls across the aisle counting his plates at lunch. Rinnie lay in a house across the lake he couldn't see from here, her window empty and her blue ribbon wound around Gwenith's fingers. And every boy on this island wore a ring like that one, and any one of their fathers could have signed the order to seal the street and burn the sick, then sat down to tea after, sipping from painted porcelain and laughing on cue while the black smoke rolled up out of the Greys.

He pressed it down, the way he'd pressed his hunger down through eight winters, and kept walking. The tittering followed him through the next arch, and the arch after that.

The bell tolled again, a single bronze note that hung shivering in the warm air and faded.

All around him the island rose from its tables. Chairs scraped back over flagstones. Students brushed crumbs from their skirts and blazers, the boys bowing from the waist and the girls sinking into curtseys, cheeks kissed and promises called across the courtyards to meet again tomorrow. The Maids stepped out from their corners and began gathering the cups.

Dusty's steps dragged to a crawl.

Half past four, tea over, and he'd never found her. She'd waited for him. He'd seen it in how her hand had hung halfway to his sleeve before she asked, and in the gold that had flooded her face when he said yes. She was the only soul on this island who'd waved him over when every other stool was taken, and she'd sat alone in a blue courtyard somewhere with the good cakes going stale, watching the arch.

"Why would a girl like that even want to have tea with me?" he muttered.

A Maid passing with a tray of empty cups didn't turn her head.

She laughed like she'd never run short of breath. She talked to jars, and drew Professor Brannoch doing push-ups in her margins. She should have had a whole table of friends beneath a canopy of her own, not a first year from the Greys who couldn't read his own notes. He thought of the empty stools crowding her elbows in the potions hall, and the two girls against the shelf who had chosen to stand.

Dusty let out a long breath and turned his head to the left.

The wall of the passage beside him was painted navy blue, deep as the lake an hour after sunset and trimmed along its top in white. It ran narrow between two buildings, and he'd walked past its mouth twice.

He took the passage at a jog.

It opened into a courtyard no larger than his room at the Silver Swan, walled in the same deep blue on every side, the noise of the island falling away behind him. At its center a stone swan rose from a shallow basin, carved so fine he could see each feather laid over the next, its long neck arched and its wings half-lifted, the marble at their edges so thin the afternoon light glowed through it like lamplight through oiled paper. A stiff wind might have cracked it. Water bubbled up beneath its breast and gurgled over the basin's lip into a narrow channel cut through the flagstones. Rose bushes climbed the walls in tall rows, heavy with fat white blooms clustered in threes and fours, their scent thick and sweet in the still air.

Beside the basin sat a curved concrete bench and a small concrete table. On the table waited a teapot beneath a quilted cozy, two porcelain cups, and a plate of small iced cakes arranged in a careful ring, one of each color. Tea stood cold in one cup. The other sat clean and dry in its saucer.

Auriel sat on the bench with her shoulders curled forward and both hands pressed over her face. Her honey-gold hair spilled around her fingers, and the red sash had slipped loose at her waist. She made no sound. Her shoulders shook, and a tear slid from beneath her palm, ran down her wrist, and dropped, darkening a small round spot on the white wool of her blazer.

Dusty stopped at the mouth of the passage. His jaw unclenched and his fists hung open at his sides, the tittering laughter and the gold rings and the black smoke over the Greys draining out of him, until he stood in the doorway of the blue courtyard with the satchel strap slack in one hand, the roses breathing sweet around him, and his eyes fixed on the girl weeping over two cups of tea.

***
Dusty cleared his throat. It scraped out rough and too loud over the gurgle of the basin, and he scuffed his boot against the flagstones to make certain of it.

Auriel jerked upright as though a switch had cracked across her back. Her hands flew from her face, and her amber eyes found him in the mouth of the passage, red-rimmed and swimming, wet tracks shining down both round cheeks.

"Oh!" She scrubbed the heel of one hand across her cheek, then the other, smearing more than she wiped. "Oh, it's — hello! The roses. I was just — the roses are very strong today. They make my eyes water. They do that. Every year."

Dusty walked into the courtyard, but not to her. He turned his shoulder to the bench and stepped to the nearest bush along the navy wall, bending until his face hung level with a cluster of four fat white blooms, and breathed in slow with his back to her.

Behind him came a small wet sniff and the whisper of white wool dragged across a face.

"They're — they're called Moonsilk." Her voice wobbled on the first word and snagged on the second. "The gardeners brought them over from the eastern beds. Years ago. Before I — before I came. Nobody comes out here to smell them, which is a crime, honestly, because they're the best on the whole island." Her next breath came steadier. "Do you smell the honey in them? Right at the bottom? Most roses just smell like roses, but these smell like honey and rain and — and clean sheets, a little. Like someone just made your bed. Don't you think?"

Dusty breathed in again, the cool petals brushing the tip of his nose, soft as Rinnie's cheek against his collar on the long winter nights. Beneath the sweetness lay something warm and golden, like the crust of Old Tobin's bread fresh from the oven.

"They smell like honey," he said.

"They do!" The brightness had crept back into her voice, most of it. "I told you. Nobody ever believes me."

He straightened and turned. She'd tucked her honey-gold hair behind her ears and knotted the red sash back into its crooked bow, and aside from the pink rims of her eyes and the damp spot darkening her white lapel, she might have been the same girl who'd waved him over in the potions hall.

Dusty bowed from the waist, low, as he'd bowed to Gwenith in the Silver Swan.

"I'm sorry I'm late," he said. "I had trouble finding the courtyard. I don't know my way around yet, and I walked past the passage twice."

"No, no, no, I'm sorry." Auriel shot to her feet, her hands fluttering in the air between them. "I always do that. I always tell people to meet me here, because it's quiet, and nobody comes out here, and it's — it's awkward, sometimes. When people see me talking to someone. They look." Her smile flickered and held. "So I tell people to come here. And then I run off before I tell them how to get here. Every time. I'm terrible."

Dusty studied her before he nodded. He'd seen them looking in the potions hall, the two girls with their heads bent together and the knot of third years by the shelves.

"I should have stopped you," he said, "before you ran off. I should have asked."

"You couldn't have. I was gone. I'm very fast when I'm excited." Her hands clasped in front of her and wrung, once. "I'm sorry. Again. I was just so excited that someone said yes. It's been a while since — well. People do say yes, at first. They come for a while." Her voice thinned. "And then after a while they stop coming. They get busy. It happens. It's fine."

She smiled up at him, bright and wide, and held it a breath too long.

Dusty didn't smile back. He turned it over and couldn't make it sit right. She'd called him over when no one else would, and drawn Brannoch sweating through push-ups in her margins, and set out two cups and a ring of iced cakes, and sat alone for half an hour waiting on a boy from the Greys she'd known a single afternoon. Nobody walked away from that. Something else lay underneath it, something no one had told him.

He'd known her three hours. In the Greys the widest smiles had most often hidden a blade up the sleeve, and though she didn't look as if she'd ever held a knife, he'd misjudged people before and paid for it in blood. He'd go careful.

"Let's stop apologizing to each other," he said. "And maybe have some tea."

Her face opened like a shutter flung back on a sunny morning. Dusty gestured to the curved bench and she sank back onto it, and he moved to take the far end—

A soft cough sounded behind him.

A Maid stood in the mouth of the passage, a head shorter than Auriel and slight as a reed in her black dress and white apron, her copper-red hair twisted into a knot so tight it drew at the corners of her eyes. Those eyes were a pale clear blue, the color of lake ice. Dusty hadn't heard her come, not a heel, not a rustle of skirts.

"Pardon the interruption." Her voice was light and pleasant. "Tea has concluded for the day, Miss Landsdon. I'm here to collect the service."

Auriel's smile slipped. "Oh. Maid Sorrel. Of course. Only — he just arrived, you see, he couldn't find the courtyard, and we haven't even poured his cup yet. Could we have a few minutes more? Only a few. Please? I'd be so grateful."

"I'm sorry, Miss Landsdon." Sorrel crossed the flagstones and bent over the table, her lace-gloved hands already lifting the quilted cozy from the pot. "Tea is at four. It ends at half past. The rule is the same for every student."

"Of course. No, you're right. Of course it is."

Auriel's hands folded in her lap. Sorrel stacked the clean saucer onto the used one and nested the cups with a small porcelain click, laying them on a silver tray Dusty hadn't seen her carry in. Her gloved fingers paused over the ring of iced cakes, and her pale eyes lifted to Auriel.

"Tomorrow is a fresh day, Miss Landsdon," she said. "Tea will be served at four, as always. And perhaps this fine young gentleman could join you here again. Should he find his way a little faster."

Auriel's head snapped up. She turned to Dusty, her round face lit gold and hopeful, her fingers knotted white in her lap.

Dusty looked back at her. He thought of the blade he'd been hunting for up her sleeve, and of the dry cup, and of the tear soaking into her lapel, and he let the first go.

"I'd like that," he said. "I'd like your company tomorrow. If you'll have me."

"Yes!" Auriel sprang up so fast the bench scraped. "Yes. Tomorrow. Four o'clock. Here. It's this passage, the blue one, you know it now, you'll remember it, won't you? I'll get here early and save the good cakes. The pink ones. You'll love the pink ones."

She snatched up her books and satchel, hugged them to her chest, and backed toward the passage, waving her free hand at him over the stack.

"Tomorrow! Don't forget! Thank you, Maid Sorrel! Goodbye, Dusty!"

Her red sash whipped around the corner, and her quick footsteps faded down the blue passage.

Dusty shook his head, the corner of his mouth tugging upward. She left rooms the way sparrows left a gutter when a cart came through, all at once and in a flurry. He hitched his satchel higher on his shoulder and turned for the passage.

"It's good to see someone talking to Miss Landsdon."

Sorrel had not looked up. She lifted the iced cakes one at a time from their plate and laid them in a lidded tin, her gloved fingers neat as a baker's boxing loaves.

"I do wonder," she said, "how long you'll last."

Dusty turned back, his black eyes narrowing.

"What do you mean by that?"

Sorrel laid the last cake in the tin and pressed the lid down. Then she looked at him, her pale eyes resting on his face without a flicker, until the gurgle of the basin had filled the whole silence.

"You'll learn," she said. "Like the rest of her so-called friends."

Dusty stepped toward her, his long shadow spilling across the table and the silver tray and the Maid's narrow black shoulders.

"Could you be more specific, please."

Sorrel set the tin on the tray and turned to face him fully, hands clasping at her waist over the white of her apron, chin tipped up to hold his gaze from a head and a half below. Her face was serene, too serene, calm as the black water that pooled in the Greys over the buried river, so still you never knew how deep it ran until somebody went under.

Dusty said nothing. He looked down at her and waited.

She smiled, a small curve of her thin lips that left her pale eyes cold as the lake in winter, and there was cruelty tucked into the corner of it.

"You should pick your battles more carefully, young man," she said. "Trying to intimidate a Maid on your first day is not a very good idea."

"Maids are supposed to be neutral."

"We are." Her smile never moved. "But we have eyes. And ears."

Dusty stepped back. He let a slow breath out through his nose, his shoulders easing down, the manners he'd worn since the lower market settling back over him as snug and stiff as his new blazer.

"I'm sorry," he said. "I don't like people trying to scare me off."

"I wasn't." Sorrel turned back to the table. "I only know that people tend to stay away from Miss Landsdon. I was wondering if you would too."

Dusty opened his mouth, but she was already lifting the teapot onto the tray and folding the quilted cozy into a neat square, her back to him.

"Why do they stay away from her?"

She smoothed the cozy flat.

"Who's telling them to stay away?"

She laid the cozy beside the pot.

"Is it because of the sash? Or her family? If there's something I ought to know—"

Sorrel lifted the tray, pot and cups and tin and cozy, and it didn't so much as tremble in her small gloved hands. She stepped around him as though he were one more rose bush along the wall and walked into the blue passage, her heels silent on the flagstones, and never looked back.

Dusty stood alone with the swan and the gurgling water and the white roses breathing honey into the still air. His hands had curled into fists again. He opened them.

The Maid had wanted him to see something. She'd come to clear the tea and handed Auriel tomorrow like a gift wrapped in lace, then turned around and tried to snatch it back with that small cold smile, and he couldn't fit the two halves together.

He started down the passage toward the dormitories, the navy walls pressing close on either side.

Rinnie had come back from the women's room at the Gilded Pheasant reciting what Maedelyn had told her in that low, clipped voice: Beauty, Elegance, and Dignity above all else. The Maids' own creed, the one they held themselves to while they served the tea and watched the tables and kept an island full of princes in line. And every student had walked across the bridge to be told that here, everyone stood equal. But Maids had eyes and ears. Maids had smiles like Sorrel's. They were people, the same as the guards who'd sealed off his street had been people, and the rules on this island were beginning to look like the rules back home, carved deep in white stone and bent whenever someone with the right ring, or the right apron, wanted them bent.

He'd add it to the list, though he no longer believed it was a list Valia could answer.

***
Dinner was a larger affair than Dusty had expected.

Ten dining halls stood scattered across the island, according to a boy on the dormitory stairs, their doors open from six until nine. The hall nearest the northern dormitories stretched long and high-beamed beneath iron chandeliers crowded with fat beeswax candles, their yellow light warm across walls paneled in dark walnut. The noise struck Dusty in the doorway, a hundred conversations rolling over the clatter of silver on porcelain, laughter surging and breaking like the river against the Helvanthor docks.

The stiff backs and cutting glances of the tea courtyards had gone slack. Blazers hung open over black undershirts, ties loosened and yanked askew, and more than one boy had shed his jacket altogether and slung it over his chair. The girls had set aside the heaviest of their gold, wrists and throats bare where at four o'clock they had glittered, their hair tumbling loose from its pins. Nobles sprawled with their elbows on the tables and laughed with their mouths wide open, loud and careless, and nobody glanced around to see who laughed with them.

The buffet ran the length of the far wall, silver chafing dishes steaming over little blue flames down a table as long as a Helvanthor street. A cook in a white cap carved rare beef into thin slices that bled pink across the board. Pheasants glistened beneath honey and thyme, and a shoulder of lamb slumped from its bone in a pool of rosemary and garlic, and at the far end tarts and custards and glazed fruit shone bright as lacquer beneath the candles.

Dusty went down the line twice and came away with four plates, two balanced along one forearm and one in each hand, the way he'd hauled crates of salt fish off the barges. He found a small table wedged beneath a window in the far corner, its two chairs pushed against the wall, and set the plates down without spilling a drop of gravy. He took the chair with its back to the walnut, where the whole room lay open before him.

He'd filled his plates with meat this time. He'd eaten bread and potatoes at lunch, and his body had been crying out for something with blood in it since the barber's scissors. He cut into the beef and ate it slow, salt and juice running rich across his tongue, then the pheasant, its skin crackling sweet between his teeth. He'd eaten more these past five days than in a whole season in the Greys. Rinnie would have laughed at him.

With the first plate cleaned and the second begun, he dragged his satchel onto his lap and pulled out the notebook, laying it open between the plates. He spread the loose pages of history beside it and smoothed them flat with the side of his hand, keeping them clear of the sauce.

He started at the top of the first page, his lips shaping each sound without a breath behind it. The B pressed his lips together, the R growled low like a dog in an alley, the A opened his mouth wide, and the N hummed behind his teeth, and there on the page sat Brannoch, the booming voice pinned into four crooked letters. He copied it again beneath, cleaner and straighter. MARTIAL he'd spelled with an S the first time and had to scratch out, and ELEMENTAL ran so long he lost his place in its belly and had to start again from the E.

It went slow, one symbol at a time, his pencil scratching and his eyes squinting in the candlelight. He'd get three words clean and run headlong into a fourth that sounded like nothing he'd ever heard spoken, a snarl of letters that swam the longer he stared. He circled it and moved on. A tutor would have untangled it in a breath. He circled another.

He'd finished his fourth plate and was halfway down the second page of history when the candlelight across his table dimmed beneath a crowd of shadows that did not move on.

Dusty looked up.

Five girls stood in a loose half circle at his table, and behind them loomed two boys so large they blotted out the chandelier.

The nearer boy stood as tall as Dusty and twice as thick through the chest, his scalp shaved to dark stubble, his nose flattened and bent where it had been broken and badly set, a white scar splitting his upper lip. The other was blond and square, his jaw like a block of quarried stone, heavy brows jutting over small pale eyes set too close together. They were the only two in the hall with their blazers buttoned to the throat and their ties knotted tight, and they stood with their hands clasped loosely before them, the way the bruisers outside the gambling dens in the Greys had stood waiting for someone to make trouble.

The girl at the center of the five stepped forward.

Where Auriel was gold and soft and short, this girl was tall and slim and straight as a sapling, her blazer the pale gray of morning mist fitted neat across her narrow shoulders. Her hair was a deep glossy black, drawn back from a smooth high brow and pinned in a coil at her nape without a strand astray. Her face was long and fine-boned, her nose small and straight, her eyes a cool clear gray like rain on slate. Her hands rested folded before her, perfectly still.

Pinned to her lapel above her heart gleamed a small golden sun, its rays thrusting out in sharp uneven spikes.

The same sun winked gold on every girl behind her, and on both of the boys. Dusty had last seen it crowning the gold-tipped spires of the cathedral in the Devout Quarter, and stamped on the swinging censers of the cream-robed priests.

"Good evening," the girl said, her voice soft and warm as water poured slow from a pitcher. "I hope we aren't disturbing your meal."

"Good evening." Dusty set down his pencil. "I've finished. I'm Dusty." He nodded toward the empty chair across from him, and then at the two more at the neighboring table, set against the wall. "Please. Sit, if you'd like."

"How kind. Thank you, Dusty."

She sat across from him, smoothing her pleated skirt beneath her as she lowered herself, and the four girls drew chairs over from the next table and arranged themselves around her. The two boys stayed on their feet, one at each of her shoulders, their eyes fixed on Dusty.

Dusty let them stare and turned to the girl across from him.

"My name is Rariel," she said. "And these are my friends. We're all affiliated with the Church, as you can see." Two slender fingers touched the golden sun on her lapel. "We like to meet the new students. Especially those who may not have many others to show them the way."

"Are you a saintess?" Dusty asked.

Rariel's smile was gentle and patient. "I am. I was brought into the Church's care when I was very small, and I've been trained all my life to share the warmth and love of the gods and the angels above with the rest of the world. It is my greatest joy."

The girl in the Devout Quarter drifted up behind Dusty's eyes, white silk and near-white hair and her gaze cast down on the cobbles, a hunched old priest leading her by the wrist through a hollow square of guards in white and gold enamel like a calf to market. She looks tired, Rinnie had whispered against his neck.

Rariel's gray eyes were clear and bright, her skin smooth, not a shadow beneath her lashes.

"And you, Dusty?" Rariel tilted her head. "Where do you come from? How are you finding your classes?"

Dusty's eyes slid to the two boys. The one with the broken nose had shifted his weight onto his toes. The blond one hadn't moved at all.

"I come from a poor background," Dusty said. "And I'm studying just fine."

Rariel's gaze dropped to the notebook lying open among the empty plates and the history pages spread beside it, taking in the circles and the crossed-out letters, the words copied two and three times over in square block print like a child's slate.

"I can see you're working very hard," she said softly. "Letters can be such a struggle, when no one has ever taken the time to teach you." She turned to the girl on her left. "Terra here might be able to help you. She's a wonderful student. And she comes from a background not so very different from your own. I think you would find a great deal in common, you and she. You could share your experiences."

The girl on her left sat up straighter, plain and round-faced, her brown hair in a tidy braid, her brown eyes wide. She offered Dusty a small, uncertain smile.

"Thank you," Dusty said. "That's a kind offer."

His eyes flicked once more to the two boys, then settled on Terra.

"Did you come from a district so poor you had to fight other starving children who tried to steal your dinner?" he asked. "Or from somewhere they dumped the sick, because nobody would pay to heal them? And then burned them alive, when the nobles got nervous about an outbreak?"

Terra's mouth opened and shut, a flush creeping up her round cheeks.

"I— no, I— my father's a miller. In Brackenford. It's a— it's a country town." Her eyes darted to Rariel. "We weren't rich. We had a bad winter, once. When I was little."

"I am so terribly sorry," Rariel said. She reached across the table as though to touch his hand and stopped just short, her fingertips coming to rest on the edge of his notebook, her gray eyes soft with pity. "That you were forced to live such a horrible life. No child should ever have to know such things. I'm glad, truly glad, that you were able to escape such a dreary upbringing."

Dusty looked at her pale fingers resting on his crooked letters.

"Does the Church ever go into those places?" he asked. "Do the saintesses? To help?" He lifted his eyes to hers. "Or does the Church only help the people who have gold to spare?"

The table went silent.

Two of the girls sucked in sharp breaths. One pressed her fingertips to her lips, her eyes gone round, and another gaped at him as though he'd spat across the tablecloth. Terra had gone white as milk.

Rariel's fingers stayed resting on his notebook, and her smile held without a tremor.

"You carry so much anger," she said gently. "It's understandable, after what you've suffered. But you should channel that anger into more fruitful endeavors, Dusty. Into learning the love of the gods and the angels. Rather than letting your misfortune cloud your mind."

Dusty's fingers closed around his pencil until the wood creaked.

Rinnie curled beneath a threadbare blanket in the corner of a tenement where the water ran black down the walls, her breath rattling in the dark, his glowing palm pressed flat to her back from dusk until dawn. And every morning the cathedral bells rolling out over the Greys for prayers, and not one priest coming down the hill except to mutter over the bodies stacked on the carts.

He laid the pencil on the table with great care, so it wouldn't roll.

"I'm sorry," he said, "that my view of the world is so different from your own. But I think it would be best if you left now. And we ended this conversation."

"I only wanted to speak with you for a little while."

Dusty met her gray eyes.

"With respect, my lady," he said, pleasantly, "please fuck off."

A girl gasped aloud. The blond boy took a half step forward, his heavy brows crashing down.

Rariel raised one hand without turning her head, and the boy stopped.

"There's no need for such language," she said. "We came in friendship."

Dusty's gaze climbed the blond boy's broad chest to his quarried jaw, crossed to the one with the broken nose, and came back down to Rariel.

"Then you should stop pretending," he said. "You come to my table with talk of love and joy. And you bring those two with you, meant to frighten me." He tipped his head toward them. "I'd guess they're both very good at martial magic. That they could put me on the floor before I got out of my chair."

"They're here for my protection," Rariel said. "Mine and my friends'. Nothing more."

"I'm a first year," Dusty said, his voice level and courteous, the same voice he'd once used to ask the baker's wife whether she'd seen the boy who'd lifted a loaf from Tobin's stall. "From the slums of a city nobody here has ever set foot in. I barely know how to use my magic. You knew all of that before you walked over here. So these two monkeys"—the boy with the broken nose flushed red to the scar on his lip—"are here to scare me."

He leaned forward, his forearms resting on the table among the empty plates.

"I watched my parents burned alive by men with magic," he said, quietly. "Those two don't scare me. And I know the moment either of them lifts a hand to me, a Maid will be standing between us before his fist lands."

The silence spread from the little corner table to the ones nearest it, heads turning, forks hovering. Rariel's smile had not left her face, but it had drawn tight at its corners, and a faint crease had carved itself between her smooth black brows.

"I would like this conversation to end," Dusty said. "I was polite to you at first. But you couldn't take the hint."

"You're the one who needs to take a hint," snapped a sharp-faced redhead at the end of the table, her golden sun winking in the candlelight.

Rariel raised her hand again, and the redhead's mouth snapped shut. Behind Rariel both boys had leaned forward onto their toes, and now they rocked back onto their heels.

Rariel drew a slow breath and let it out. When she looked at Dusty again her gray eyes were soft and sad.

"You have been a victim your whole life," she said. "I see that. And I hope that one day you will listen, and follow the precepts of the gods and the angels, so that your life can hold more meaning than it has." She folded her hands on the table. "I only came here tonight to offer you a better friendship than the one you've been receiving."

Dusty blinked. "I'm sorry?"

"Terra," Rariel said, and the plain girl beside her jumped as though pinched, "would be glad to make your acquaintance. To help you with your letters, and to show you the island, and to sit with you at tea." Terra's round face held no gladness at all, her eyes fixed hard on the tablecloth. "In place of the acquaintance you've made with Auriel."

The empty stools in the potions hall rose up in Dusty's mind, three on the left and two on the right, and the girls against the shelves who'd chosen to stand. People do say yes, at first, and then after a while they stop coming. Two cups on a concrete table beneath the stone swan, and only one of them poured.

Dusty sat back in his chair.

"I'll decline your offer," he said.

"Associating with Auriel will not go well," Rariel said. "For anyone."

"I'll decline your offer," Dusty said again, the words heavier.

"You don't understand." Rariel leaned forward, her voice low and earnest, patient as a nursemaid explaining why the stove is hot. "It isn't about her. Not as a student, and not as a person. Auriel failed her training. She failed as a saintess. The Church enrolled her here so that she might learn other ways to serve, since she could not serve in the way she was meant to."

"I decline."

"She must learn to love the whole," Rariel went on, gently, as though he hadn't spoken. "Everyone, all at once, equally. As the gods love us. Not this—" her fine nose wrinkled, the first crack in the smooth porcelain of her face, "—this narrow, individual love she clings to. It's for her own good that she learns that lesson. And if she learns it, her friends will return to her."

Dusty stood.

He closed the notebook and slid it into his satchel, gathered the loose pages of history and squared their edges against the tabletop before tucking them in after it, and slipped the pencil into his breast pocket. He buckled the satchel and slung its strap across his chest, his hands steady on the leather.

"Do you understand, Dusty?" Rariel asked.

The two boys loomed behind her, their shadows stretching long across the table and its four empty plates.

Dusty looked down at her, his jaw aching where his teeth had locked.

"I must take my leave," he said, the words grinding out between his teeth. "Because if I continue this conversation, my lady, I will say a great many things that would offend your so-called gods and angels."

He turned and walked away down the long candlelit hall, past the tables of sprawling nobles and the steaming silver of the buffet. In the corner beside the doors a Maid stood still as carved stone, hands folded over her white apron, and her eyes followed him out.

He did not look back.
